\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 The Digest\b0\par\par\uc2 By Amanda Holpuch and Stanley Reed \par \uc2 532 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 September 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 LITIGATION\par \par \uc2 Nintendo\uc2  and Pok\u195\'00\'C3\u169\'00\'A9mon File a Patent Lawsuit\par \par \uc2 Nintendo\uc2  and the Pok\u195\'00\'C3\u169\'00\'A9mon Company on Wednesday filed a patent infringement lawsuit against the video game company that developed Palword, a game with cute creatures that resemble the characters in Pok\u195\'00\'C3\u169\'00\'A9mon, except they use military-grade weapons.\par \par \uc2 Palworld became one of the most popular games in the world when it was released in January. It had been promoted for three years before that, and the trailers for it inspired memes and other forms of mocking by people who thought the game looked like an apocalyptic version of Pok\u195\'00\'C3\u169\'00\'A9mon.\par \par \uc2 Palworld's makers have repeatedly draw distinctions between Palworld, which is a survival game, and Pok\u195\'00\'C3\u169\'00\'A9mon, where players collect monsters and battle.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, the Pok\u195\'00\'C3\u169\'00\'A9mon Company and \uc2 Nintendo\uc2 , a joint owner of the Pok\u195\'00\'C3\u169\'00\'A9mon Company, said in a statement that the companies had filed a suit in Tokyo District Court against the maker of Palworld, Pocketpair. The suit accuses Palword of violating ''multiple patent rights'' and seeks compensation for damages, according to the statement.\par \par \uc2 Pocketpair, which is based in Tokyo, said in a statement on Thursday that it did not know the details of the companies' claims. ''At this moment, we are unaware of the specific patents we are accused of infringing upon, and we have not been notified of such details,'' the statement said.\par \par \uc2 Pocketpair said that the success of Palword had been a surprise and that the company would continue doing work to improve the game. AMANDA HOLPUCH\par \par \uc2 COMPANIES\par \par \uc2 GE Vernova\uc2  Downsizing Offshore Wind Business\par \par \uc2 The energy company \uc2 GE Vernova\uc2  said on Friday that it would pare back its troubled offshore wind business, which has been hit with financial losses and accidents. The changes, which are aimed at coping with a difficult environment for the wind industry, could result in around 900 job losses.\par \par \uc2 The company's struggles are a blow to the offshore wind industry, which is a major source of electric power in northern Europe and is thought to have potential to supply large volumes of green energy to the East Coast of the United States.\par \par \uc2 A substantial downsizing of \uc2 GE Vernova\uc2  would leave just two main western players, Siemens Gamesa of Germany and \uc2 Vestas Wind Systems\uc2  of Demark -- a situation that could leave the industry short of construction capacity and could push equipment prices higher, raising consumers' bills.\par \par \uc2 GE Vernova\uc2 , a spinoff from the venerable \uc2 General Electric\uc2  industrial conglomerate, said it had presented a proposal on Thursday for reducing the company's size to its European Works Council, which represents its workers, a necessary step before job cuts.\par \par \uc2 The company said in an emailed statement that its new plan reflected ''industrywide challenges for wind'' and aimed to create ''a smaller, leaner and more profitable business.''\par \par \uc2 The company declined to specify which countries' operations would be hit hardest.\par \par \uc2 A leader in land-based wind turbines, \uc2 GE Vernova\uc2  hustled to develop a giant turbine called the Haliade-X for offshore deployment, spending $400 million. STANLEY REED\par \par \uc2 (PHOTOGRAPH BY RANDI BAIRD FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page B