\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Boeing\uc2  Seeks Mediators To Advance Labor Talks\b0\par\par\uc2 By Niraj Chokshi \par \uc2 940 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 September 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 6\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Federal mediators are helping the aerospace manufacturer and leaders of the union representing more than 33,000 workers who went on strike last week reach a deal.\par \par \uc2 Boeing\uc2  and its largest union restarted contract negotiations on Tuesday with the help of federal mediators, days after thousands of workers rejected a previous offer and went on strike.\par \par \uc2 The walkout, which began on Friday, has already drastically slowed the company's production of commercial airplanes because most of the union's more than 33,000 members work in manufacturing in the Seattle area. The company makes several airplanes, including the 737 Max, its most popular, in two factories outside the city.\par \par \uc2 If the strike lasts for several weeks, analysts estimate that \uc2 Boeing\uc2 , which was already in a financially precarious position before the stoppage began, could lose billions of dollars.\par \par \uc2 Workers last week overwhelmingly rejected a tentative contract that \uc2 Boeing\uc2  and union officials had negotiated, saying that it fell well short of what the union had initially sought on raises, retirement benefits and other issues. But in a message to employees on Monday, \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's chief financial officer, \uc2 Brian West\uc2 , said that the company was ''working in good faith'' on a new deal that reflected worker feedback and allowed the company to return to normal operations.\par \par \uc2 Tuesday's meeting took place in Seattle, where small teams from \uc2 Boeing\uc2  and the union convened with representatives from the \uc2 Federal Mediation and Conciliation Service\uc2 , a government agency that helps to resolve labor disputes.\par \par \uc2 The agency's role in such mediations is narrow. It does not make decisions on the content or outcome of talks, but aims to help the parties find an agreeable resolution, Javier Ramirez, the agency's deputy director of field operations, said in an interview on Monday.\par \par \uc2 ''We focus more on the process and helping the parties explore the art of the possible,'' said Mr. Ramirez, who is in charge of the agency's 130 mediators.\par \par \uc2 He and two colleagues, Beth Schindler and Myla Hite, took part in the negotiations on Tuesday. Predicting the outcome of such discussions is difficult, and negotiations are often fluid, Mr. Ramirez said.\par \par \uc2 ''We look at demeanor, the preparedness, the willingness, there's so many things that factor into how we operate that we just have to constantly be reading the room, and that's where that art comes in,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Union leaders on Saturday encouraged their members to speak out: ''Now is the moment to rise. Show \uc2 Boeing\uc2  that our voices aren't just loud; they are unstoppable,'' the negotiating committee said in a message, encouraging members to keep picket lines active around the clock.\par \par \uc2 In a message before the vote, union leaders had supported the now-rejected deal, calling it ''the best contract we've negotiated in our history.'' The talks that led to that proposal were informed by surveys of, suggestions from and meetings with members. On Sunday, the union said it had shared a new survey to gather the top priorities of its members.\par \par \uc2 For \uc2 Boeing\uc2 , the consequences of the walkout will depend on its length.\par \par \uc2 The union's last strike, in 2008, lasted nearly two months. \uc2 Boeing\uc2  said in securities filings at the time that because of the strike, it delivered 104 fewer planes than expected that year, and its annual revenue dropped $6.4 billion.\par \par \uc2 Boeing\uc2  has since moved assembly of its 787 Dreamliner to a nonunion plant in North Charleston, S.C. If the current strike lasts as long as the previous one, it could still cost \uc2 Boeing\uc2  at least $3 billion, according to an estimate from \uc2 Cai von Rumohr\uc2 , a research analyst at the investment bank TD Cowen.\par \par \uc2 In his message on Monday, Mr. West, the chief financial officer, said that \uc2 Boeing\uc2  would establish cost-cutting measures, including freezing hiring and nonessential travel. The company is also considering temporary furloughs and planning ''significant'' cuts to purchases from suppliers, he said.\par \par \uc2 Most of the workers covered by the talks are represented by District 751 of the machinists' union in Seattle. A small number work at a parts plant in Portland, Ore., and are represented by District W24 of the union.\par \par \uc2 Boeing\uc2  employs more than 66,000 people in Washington State, according to the Seattle Metropolitan Chamber of Commerce. The company supports more than twice as many jobs when considering its effect on other businesses.\par \par \uc2 When negotiations began in March, workers were resentful of the loss of pension benefits a decade ago, which the union had sought to reinstate, and the decision to move the assembly of the 787 from Washington to South Carolina. At the time, the union said it was seeking a 40 percent raise.\par \par \uc2 The offer that the workers rejected last week included a 25 percent raise, an increase in 401(k) contributions from \uc2 Boeing\uc2  and a commitment to build a new commercial plane in the Seattle area.\par \par \uc2 The strike is the first big public test for \uc2 Kelly Ortberg\uc2 , who became \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's chief executive last month.\par \par \uc2 The company's deliveries of the 737 Max were rising last year after the pandemic disrupted its operations and supply chain. But that recovery was derailed when a panel blew off one of the jets during an \uc2 Alaska Airlines\uc2  flight in January. The episode renewed concerns about \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's commitment to quality and safety five years after two fatal crashes involving the 737 Max.\par \par \uc2 More than 33,000 workers who make planes for Boeing went on strike on Friday after rejecting a tentative contract. (PHOTOGRAPH BY LINDSEY WASSON/ASSOCIATED PRESS) This article appeared in print on page B6. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240918ek9i0003o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
