\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Boeing\uc2  to Freeze Hiring and Cut Spending Amid Strike\b0\par\par\uc2 By Niraj Chokshi \par \uc2 473 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 September 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 More than 33,000 \uc2 Boeing\uc2  workers went on strike last week after rejecting a tentative contract the company had negotiated with their unions.\par \par \uc2 Days after thousands of employees went on strike, \uc2 Boeing\uc2  said on Monday that it would freeze hiring and cut spending, halting production of its most popular commercial jet.\par \par \uc2 Union leaders called for more than 33,000 \uc2 Boeing\uc2  workers to go on strike on Friday, after members voted overwhelmingly to reject a tentative contract negotiated by their union and company management. The walkout brought production of the 737 Max and other planes to a virtual halt, hampering \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's recovery from a safety crisis after a panel blew off one of its planes during a passenger flight in January.\par \par \uc2 The company is ''working in good faith to reach a new contract agreement,'' but needs to take steps to rein in spending during the walkout, \uc2 Brian West\uc2 , \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's chief financial officer, wrote in a message to employees on Monday.\par \par \uc2 ''Our business is in a difficult period,'' he said. ''This strike jeopardizes our recovery in a significant way and we must take necessary actions to preserve cash and safeguard our shared future.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. West said \uc2 Boeing\uc2  would protect funding for safety, quality and customer support while cutting spending in other areas. In addition to pausing hiring, the company froze nonessential travel and eliminated first- and business-class air travel for top executives.\par \par \uc2 Boeing\uc2  also limited spending on consultants, advertising, marketing, charitable giving and on-site catering, and it is considering temporary furloughs for rank-and-file workers, managers and executives.\par \par \uc2 Mr. West said \uc2 Boeing\uc2  was also planning to cut supplier spending, a move that could have prolonged consequences. The aerospace supply chain is fragile, and cost-cutting can imperil smaller vendors to larger manufacturers like \uc2 Boeing\uc2  or its main rival, \uc2 Airbus\uc2 . The supplier spending cuts include stopping most orders for parts used in \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's 737, 767 and 777 airplanes, Mr. West said.\par \par \uc2 It is not clear how long the strike will last, but ending it will not be easy. In a vote on Thursday, nearly 95 percent of \uc2 Boeing\uc2  workers represented by the \uc2 International Association of Machinists and Aerospace Workers\uc2  rejected the tentative contract, while a slightly larger share voted in favor of a strike. Many members were dissatisfied by the size of raises and retirement benefits in the proposed contract.\par \par \uc2 The employees represented by the union last went on strike in 2008 for nearly two months. The contract the union ultimately reached with the company after that walkout has been extended several times before it expired at midnight on Thursday. \uc2 Boeing\uc2  and the union are scheduled to restart talks on Tuesday, with the help of federal mediators.\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page B3. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240917ek9h0003m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
