\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Rejecting Deal, Workers Leave Jobs at \uc2 Boeing\b0\par\par\uc2 By Niraj Chokshi and Danielle Kaye \par \uc2 1,276 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 September 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Thousands of union members began the first walkout at the company since 2008. Federal mediators said the company and the union would meet early next week.\par \par \uc2 Thousands of \uc2 Boeing\uc2  workers went on strike on Friday after overwhelmingly rejecting a contract their union negotiated, a potentially costly disruption to the aerospace giant as it tries to recover from a series of safety crises.\par \par \uc2 The strike, the first at \uc2 Boeing\uc2  in 16 years, brought airplane production to a halt in the Seattle area, home to most of \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's commercial plane manufacturing. The slowdown could also disrupt the company's supply chain.\par \par \uc2 Boeing\uc2  plays a substantial role in the U.S. economy. It employs almost 150,000 people across the country -- nearly half of them in Washington State -- and is one of the nation's largest exporters. The company, which also makes military jets, rockets, spacecraft and Air Force One, is a global symbol of America's manufacturing strength.\par \par \uc2 The White House said on Friday that it was in touch with \uc2 Boeing\uc2  and the union, the \uc2 International Association of Machinists and Aerospace Workers\uc2 . And on Friday evening, a federal mediation service said it would convene talks between the company and the union early next week.\par \par \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's stock tumbled 3.7 percent on Friday and has fallen nearly 40 percent this year. The company's debt rating is also in jeopardy.\par \par \uc2 Brian West\uc2 , \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's chief financial officer, said at a conference on Friday that the strike would affect production, deliveries and operations, and would ''jeopardize our recovery.''\par \par \uc2 ''We want to get back to the table,'' Mr. West added.\par \par \uc2 Union leaders and company management had reached a tentative contract agreement on Sunday after months of talks. Union leaders said it was ''the best contract we've negotiated in our history.'' But it fell short of what the union initially sought, including bigger raises, and it was rejected on Thursday by 95 percent of the membership.\par \par \uc2 A vast majority of the 33,000 workers governed by the contract are represented by District 751 of the machinists' union, \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's largest, and mostly work on commercial airplanes in the Seattle area. The dispute also involves workers in the Portland, Ore., area who are represented by the union's smaller District W24.\par \par \uc2 The union represents about a fifth of the company's more than 170,000 employees worldwide.\par \par \uc2 ''This is about respect. This is about addressing the past, and this is about fighting for our future,'' said Jon Holden, the president of District 751.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Holden had said in a statement several days before the strike that union leaders had recommended approving the deal because ''we can't guarantee we can achieve more in a strike.'' He added that the union would ''protect and support'' whatever decision the members make.\par \par \uc2 Kelly Ortberg\uc2 , the company's new chief executive, had urged employees to approve the deal. ''A strike would put our shared recovery in jeopardy, further eroding trust with our customers,'' he said in an email to workers on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Ortberg, who joined \uc2 Boeing\uc2  last month, has sought to reset the company's relationship with its workers and met with employees in the Seattle area this week to hear their thoughts about the deal. Other pressing issues include improving quality and safety, restoring the company's reputation, fixing its relationship with regulators and improving its financial position, which includes reducing its nearly $60 billion in debt.\par \par \uc2 The ratings firm \uc2 Moody's\uc2  said on Friday that it was reviewing \uc2 Boeing\uc2  for a downgrade in light of the potential impact of the strike. The plane maker's rating is currently just one rung above the ''junk'' speculative grade.\par \par \uc2 The last \uc2 Boeing\uc2  strike, in 2008, lasted 50 days; the contract that ended the dispute has been extended twice. If the current strike lasts about as long, it will cost \uc2 Boeing\uc2  at least $3 billion, according to an estimate from \uc2 Cai von Rumohr\uc2 , a research analyst at the investment bank TD Cowen.\par \par \uc2 Boeing\uc2  operates two large factories in the Seattle area: one in Renton, where it makes the 737 Max, and another in Everett, where it makes the 767 and 777.\par \par \uc2 The Max, by far \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's most popular model, accounts for more than three-fourths of the 5,490 planes the company has on order. But output at the Renton factory is far behind where \uc2 Boeing\uc2  wants. The company has been forced to slow production to make quality improvements after a panel fell off a Max being used for an \uc2 Alaska Airlines\uc2  flight in January. Eventually, \uc2 Boeing\uc2  plans to expand Max production to Everett.\par \par \uc2 The rejection of the proposal on Thursday reflects resentment among workers over concessions made in past talks, including the loss of pension benefits a decade ago, which the union had sought to reinstate. Workers and the union were also angered by the company's decision in 2020 to consolidate production of its 787 Dreamliner at a nonunion factory in South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 But many in the union were not around for those events: About half the members of District 751 have less than six years of experience at the company. Those workers generally earn less than their more experienced peers, which may make a strike financially difficult, even with the union's offer to pay members $250 per week starting in the third week of the strike.\par \par \uc2 The proposed deal would have delivered raises of 25 percent over the life of the four-year contract; the unions started the talks by asking for raises of 40 percent. Some workers who started from a lower base wage would have received bigger raises; for about 7,500 workers, pay would have increased 45 percent, while another 5,000 workers would have received raises of 53 percent, according to \uc2 Boeing\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Boeing\uc2  said the tentative contract would have raised the average salary for a machinist, excluding overtime, from nearly $76,000 to roughly $106,000 over four years.\par \par \uc2 The rejected deal also would have provided each union member with a $3,000 ratification bonus. \uc2 Boeing\uc2  had also agreed to increase annual payments to the union 401(k) plan by up to $4,160 per worker, cover more of the cost of health care, provide 12 weeks of paid parental leave and make improvements to work-life balance, including reductions in mandatory overtime. The company also agreed to build its next commercial plane in the Pacific Northwest if such an effort were undertaken in the life of the contract.\par \par \uc2 In rejecting the deal, workers may feel they have an upper hand as \uc2 Boeing\uc2  seeks to move past the crisis that began when the panel fell off a 737 Max. While no one was seriously injured in that incident, it reignited concerns about the quality and safety of \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's planes five years after two fatal Max crashes led regulators worldwide to ground the plane for nearly two years.\par \par \uc2 After the January episode, the \uc2 Federal Aviation Administration\uc2  limited production of the Max. The company has since increased inspections, added training for new hires, started to simplify procedures and limited tasks performed out of sequence, a practice known as traveled work.\par \par \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's employees may also have been more willing to vote for a strike because they were emboldened by recent walkouts involving autoworkers, screenwriters and actors.\par \par \uc2 Peter Eavis contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Peter Eavis contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Workers picketing on Friday in Renton, Wash. Thousands of machinists and aerospace workers at Boeing have gone on strike. (PHOTOGRAPH BY GRANT HINDSLEY FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (A19) This article appeared in print on page A1, A19. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240914ek9e0003v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
