\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Agreement Is Reached By \uc2 Boeing\uc2  And Unions\b0\par\par\uc2 By Niraj Chokshi \par \uc2 697 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 September 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The proposed contract, if approved by union members on Thursday, includes a 25 percent raise over four years and a promise to build the next plane in the Seattle-Portland area.\par \par \uc2 Boeing\uc2  reached a tentative deal on Sunday for a new contract with unions representing more than 33,000 workers, a significant step toward avoiding a strike that threatened to shut down production when the existing agreement expires later this week.\par \par \uc2 The proposal, which has yet to be voted on by the workers, would be the first full agreement between the company and the unions in 16 years and would deliver raises of 25 percent over the four-year life of the contract. That falls short of the 40 percent the unions had sought, but includes other victories, including improvements to health care and retirement benefits, and a promise to build the company's next commercial airplane in the Seattle and Portland regions.\par \par \uc2 If approved, the deal would resolve one of the most pressing items on the agenda of \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's new chief executive, \uc2 Kelly Ortberg\uc2 , who started a month ago. Mr. Ortberg, the former head of aerospace supplier \uc2 Rockwell Collins\uc2 , inherited a company in crisis after a panel blew off a 737 Max jet in January. Though no one was seriously injured, that episode reignited concerns from five years ago about the quality and safety of \uc2 Boeing\uc2  planes after two fatal crashes involving Max planes. It also shed light on \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's shortcomings on quality control.\par \par \uc2 The leadership of the unions -- District 751 and the much smaller District W24 of the \uc2 International Association of Machinists and Aerospace Workers\uc2  -- urged their members to approve the deal.\par \par \uc2 ''Negotiations are a give and take, and although there was no way to achieve success on every single item, we can honestly say that this proposal is the best contract we've negotiated in our history,'' Jon Holden, president of District 751, and Brandon Bryant, president of District W24, said in the joint message.\par \par \uc2 The unions mainly represent production workers who build commercial and military airplanes. District 751 is by far \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's largest union, representing the vast majority of the workers governed by the proposed and existing contracts. Many of the workers live in the Seattle area, which is home to most of \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's commercial airplane production.\par \par \uc2 The company also opened a factory more than a decade ago in South Carolina, where nonunion workers perform final assembly of its twin-aisle 787 Dreamliner plane.\par \par \uc2 Members of the unions will vote on the deal on Thursday, ahead of the midnight expiration of the current contract. The vote will include two parts: one on accepting the offer and another asking to reaffirm a nearly unanimous, but symbolic strike vote over the summer.\par \par \uc2 A strike is still possible as soon as Friday, but would require both a majority rejecting the deal and at least two-thirds reaffirming the earlier strike vote. The new contract would still take effect even if most members rejected the deal, but less than two-thirds approved a strike.\par \par \uc2 If the offer is ratified, each union member would receive $3,000 on ratification, the company said in a statement. \uc2 Boeing\uc2  would also increase annual payments into the union's 401(k) plan by up to $4,160 per worker, cover more of the cost of health care and commit to improvements in work-life balance, such as reduced mandatory overtime.\par \par \uc2 In a video message to employees, \uc2 Stephanie Pope\uc2 , the chief executive of \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's commercial airplane unit, described the deal as a ''historic offer.'' She also praised the agreement to build the company's next commercial plane in the Pacific Northwest.\par \par \uc2 ''\uc2 Boeing\uc2 's roots are here in Washington. It is where generations of workers have built incredible airplanes that connect the world,'' she said. ''And it's why we're excited that, as part of the contract, our team in the Puget Sound region will build \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's next new airplane. This would go along with our other flagship models, meaning job security for generations to come.''\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page B4. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240909ek990003h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
