\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Market Slides as Investors Deal With Shifting Bets on Rate Cuts\b0\par\par\uc2 By Joe Rennison and Danielle Kaye \par \uc2 715 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 September 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The stock market ''can't win'' at the moment, said Andrew Brenner, head of international fixed income at National Alliance Securities. ''Heads I lose, tails I lose.''\par \par \uc2 Stocks fell on Friday at the end of a bruising week for Wall Street, as investors grappled with weaker-than-expected labor market data and a warning from a \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  governor that bigger than usual rate cuts may be needed to avoid a sharp economic slowdown.\par \par \uc2 The S&P 500 fell 1.7 percent, its fourth consecutive daily decline, leaving the index down about 4.2 percent for the week, its worst week since March 2023. Other stock gauges also fared poorly, with the Nasdaq Composite dropping 2.6 percent and the Russell 2000 index of smaller companies slumping 1.6 percent.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  reported on Friday that employers added 142,000 jobs in August. That was fewer than economists expected, and the hiring numbers for June and July were also revised downward.\par \par \uc2 On the heels of fresh data, \uc2 Chris Waller\uc2 , a Fed governor, hinted at the possibility of a larger half-percentage-point cut later this month, saying that ''the current batch of data no longer requires patience, it requires action.''\par \par \uc2 With uncertainty over the economic outlook, investors have been left jittery. Stocks had steadily rallied for the first half of the year, leaving many investors sitting on healthy returns. The S&P 500 peaked in July, with a gain of over 18 percent for the year. Even after the recent pullback from those highs the index remains roughly 13 percent higher for the year.\par \par \uc2 Investors appear keen not to sacrifice gains made earlier in the year by betting that the rally could still inch a little higher: An August survey of fund managers by \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  showed that while most still expect the economy to keep growing as the Fed lowers interest rates, the level of concern over a misstep by the central bank is high.\par \par \uc2 The strength of the rally has also left little room for optimism, some investors warned. If the economy slows more quickly than expected then the Fed will likely increase the speed at which it cuts rates. Lower rates are typically positive for the stock market, but not if they are accompanied by a sharper economic slowdown. If the economy outperforms, rates are likely to stay high longer, raising the risk that the Fed will keep the brake on the economy for too long and eventually resulting in a downturn.\par \par \uc2 The stock market ''can't win'' at the moment, said Andrew Brenner, head of international fixed income at National Alliance Securities. ''Heads I lose, tails I lose,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Investors are betting that the Fed will lower interest rates by a quarter of a percentage point when the central bank meets this month, but those bets -- drawn from prices in interest-rate futures markets -- swung on Friday up toward a possible half-point cut before falling back again, pointing to investors' uncertainty over the path ahead.\par \par \uc2 ''There isn't much precedent for a move that big outside of some very obvious economic weakness or panic in the markets,'' said John Luke Tyner, an analyst at Aptus Capital Advisors. ''We're not there yet.''\par \par \uc2 September is typically a weak month for the stock market. The last time the S&P 500 rose in September was 2019. The slide this week marks the index's worst since last September.\par \par \uc2 Oil prices also fell sharply this week, a harbinger of economic gloom. Brent crude, the international benchmark, slipped to its lowest point of the year, at about $71 a barrel on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Lauren Goodwin\uc2 , an economist at New York Life Investments, said she expected the Fed to choose a smaller, quarter-point rate cut this month, partly fueled by concern that a bigger move could spook the market if it's not tied to economic data that clearly shows the economy headed into a downturn.\par \par \uc2 ''At this point, the biggest risk to the economy, which is of course what the Fed is worried about, is the market itself,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page B2. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240907ek9700010\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
