\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 A Hot Labor Market? College Grads Disagree.\b0\par\par\uc2 By Talmon Joseph Smith \par \uc2 1,339 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 September 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Unemployment is still low, but job seekers are competing for fewer openings, and hiring is sluggish. That's a big turnaround from recent years.\par \par \uc2 For much of the last three years, employers were fighting one another for workers. Now the tables have turned a bit. Few employers are firing. Layoff rates remain near record lows. But fewer employers are hiring.\par \par \uc2 That has left job seekers, employed or unemployed, competing for limited openings. And younger, less experienced applicants -- even those with freshly obtained college degrees -- have been feeling left out.\par \par \uc2 A spring survey of employers by the \uc2 National Association of Colleges and Employers\uc2  found that hiring projections for this year's college graduating class were below last year's. And it showed that finance, insurance and real estate organizations were planning a 14.5 percent decrease in hiring this year, a sharp U-turn from its 16.7 percent increase last year.\par \par \uc2 Separately, the latest report from the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  shows the overall pace of hiring in professional and business services -- a go-to for many young graduates -- is down to levels not seen since 2009.\par \par \uc2 For recent graduates, ages 22 to 27, rates of unemployment and underemployment (defined as the share of graduates working in jobs that typically do not require a college degree) have risen slightly since 2023, according to government data.\par \par \uc2 At 4.3 percent, the unemployment rate is still low. But a key measure of labor market momentum known as the hires rate, which tracks a month's hires as a share of overall employment, has notably fallen back to the sluggish pace of 2014.\par \par \uc2 ''That is historically consistent with an unemployment rate of 6.5 percent,'' said Guy Berger, director of economic research at the Burning Glass Institute, which studies the labor market. ''It's a tough time to find a job.''\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2  Careers Index, a survey of \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2  members meant to track how they are feeling about their ability to get a job and a raise, and progress in their careers, was rising until January 2023. But the compilers of the index found last month that ''workers are less confident in their career progress'' than in July 2020, just after the brief but brutal pandemic recession.\par \par \uc2 Altogether, the data depict a moment that is far from dire for most recent graduates, but still discouraging for those who have spent the past year hunting hungrily for opportunities, especially work that feels like the right fit for their education and skills.\par \par \uc2 Baily Hays, a 2023 graduate of \uc2 Pepperdine University\uc2  in Malibu, Calif., is among them. An international relations major who lives in Seattle, she found an unpaid internship at a global nonprofit, \uc2 International Justice Mission\uc2 , shortly after finishing school so she could build more experience while looking for paid work.\par \par \uc2 But even after it ended, Ms. Hays spent four months of this year unemployed, in what she called a ''grueling process.'' And she wasn't alone, she said: Other new graduates she knew spent ''six to nine months'' searching for a job, or are still looking. ''I could say for probably almost 100 percent of my friends, it was pretty horrible,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 She felt that she and many of her young peers were falling victim to ''ghost listings'' -- job listings left up by employers that are not actively being filled. According to a wide range of labor market analysts, that ghosting has not just been in their heads.\par \par \uc2 Lisa K. Simon and Stephanie Hao, economists at Revelio Labs, a company that tracks employment data, found that the ratio of completed hires to job postings had plunged recently. In 2021, there were roughly eight hires for every 10 job postings. By early this year, there were only four hires per 10 postings. (Some of this could be explained by economic uncertainty among employers, not changes in their norms, but Revelio Labs also reported a 160 percent increase since 2018 in the share of interviewees who mention receiving no reply from recruiters.)\par \par \uc2 While searching for full-time work, Ms. Hays picked up dog-sitting gigs and, recently married, was able to save by moving in with her in-laws.\par \par \uc2 When a job opportunity in human resources came her way through a mentor's reference, she jumped at it. Though it was not necessarily her first pick coming out of school, she said, she has ''totally adapted, and it's been good.''\par \par \uc2 Still, there were times she wondered about going to graduate school -- ''so I could skip the years of a bad job market,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Tim Duy\uc2 , a professor of economics at the \uc2 University of Oregon\uc2  and the chief economist at SGH Macro Advisors, a research firm, said he sympathized with the young people whose main problem in job hunting was graduating into a labor market was different from the one that existed a couple of years earlier.\par \par \uc2 ''It's not fun,'' he said, but such labor market effects are routine when the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  ''is intentionally cooling down the economy.''\par \par \uc2 After inflation soared in late 2021, the Fed responded by rapidly raising interest rates in 2022 and 2023. A large part of the Fed's stated aim was to slow down the labor market by making the cost of expanding a business less attractive. The economy has avoided an outright contraction in commercial activity, or a burst of bankruptcies. But Fed officials have largely achieved their implicit goal: suppressing the ability of many workers to obtain big raises or higher-paying jobs of their choice, which, in the view of policymakers, helps suppress inflation.\par \par \uc2 Inflation has decelerated sharply over the past year. And that has led Fed officials to signal they will soon ease up on the tight financial screws their rate policies put on many businesses. That is great news for recent graduates, and those of the near future, according to Karin Kimbrough, the chief economist for \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''I talk to a lot of C-suite professionals,'' Ms. Kimbrough said. And based on those conversations she believes that businesses have a pent-up willingness to hire, but have been in limbo, anxiously waiting for the Fed to actually lower rates.\par \par \uc2 ''Companies that are waiting very much for rate cuts to materialize are also quite cautious about the economic outlook,'' she said in an interview late last month. ''In that liminal state between whether we have actually escaped a recession -- which it appears so far we have -- I just think companies have been very cautious about hiring new entrants to the job market. I don't think that's a permanent state -- I think that it'll look different next year.''\par \par \uc2 On top of experiencing anxieties induced by high interest rates, employers may have simply been avoiding large expansions or hiring waves that could end up looking like a waste of resources if a downturn materializes.\par \par \uc2 Fed officials are expected to reduce interest rates at their meeting in mid-September. Markets are pricing in a series of further rate cuts, continuing into next year.\par \par \uc2 That would substantially ease the cost of a variety of business deals and loans. And more broadly, even if the rate cuts are not large, they may still be seen as a bright greenlight that the economic expansion can continue with the Fed's blessing, and the brunt of the inflation fight behind them.\par \par \uc2 One potential knock-on effect of that shift could be a fresh wave of hiring.\par \par \uc2 ''Monetary policy does have the ability to impact employers' hiring decisions, and also their confidence in deploying more capital and launching new initiatives,'' Ms. Kimbrough of \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2  said. Once interest rates decline, she concluded, ''I think they'll feel more confident about hiring.''\par \par \uc2 Baily Hays, a 2023 graduate of Pepperdine University, spent four months of this year unemployed, in what she described as a ''grueling process.'' (PHOTOGRAPH BY CHONA KASINGER FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (B5) This article appeared in print on page B1, B5. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240906ek9600016\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
