\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 How Will Strike Affect Travel Plans for Labor Day Weekend?\b0\par\par\uc2 By Derek M. Norman \par \uc2 729 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 September 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Though hotel chains say they'll continue to operate normally, travelers could see disruptions as employees walked off the job in cities from Boston to Honolulu. Here's what to know.\par \par \uc2 Over the busy Labor Day weekend, at least 10,000 unionized front-desk workers, housekeepers and other employees at 24 hotel properties across the country walked off the job on Sunday after failing to come to an agreement in contract negotiations.\par \par \uc2 The strikes -- which as of Sunday afternoon were affecting some Hilton, \uc2 Hyatt\uc2  and \uc2 Marriott\uc2  hotels, and one Fairmont property, in Boston; San Francisco; Seattle; San Jose, Calif.; Honolulu; Kauai, Hawaii; San Diego; and Greenwich, Conn. -- were scheduled to last two to three days in each city, just as many travelers' holiday weekend plans were in full swing. More hotel workers in other cities could also soon announce strikes.\par \par \uc2 The strike comes as members of the \uc2 UNITE HERE\uc2  union at \uc2 Hilton\uc2 , \uc2 Hyatt\uc2 , \uc2 Marriott\uc2  and \uc2 Omni\uc2  properties across the country have been engaged in contentious talks since at least May.\par \par \uc2 The affected hotels say they plan to stay open, perhaps with modified services, but they may not always notify travelers that a strike is happening. So inquire ahead of time, or you may be surprised to find a spirited picket line in front of your hotel when you arrive.\par \par \uc2 Here's what else you need to know.\par \par \uc2 There's a strike at the hotel where I have a reservation. What should I do?\par \par \uc2 First, call the hotel and ask to speak to a manager to confirm that a strike is taking place at that property. \uc2 UNITE HERE\uc2  has also created a website, FairHotel.org, that offers an updated map of which properties are experiencing labor disputes. Ask the manager which services are being affected by the strike, and ask about the hotel's cancellation policy.\par \par \uc2 Then decide if you still want to stay at the hotel.\par \par \uc2 Besides weighing the effect on services, consider your feelings about the dispute itself, said \uc2 Bjorn Hanson\uc2 , an adjunct professor at the \uc2 New York University\uc2  Jonathan M. Tisch Center of Hospitality. Some travelers like to support the business and keep their reservation at the property, Dr. Hanson said. Others prefer to support the striking workers by not crossing a picket line.\par \par \uc2 If you choose not to stay at the affected hotel, he said, you should first book new accommodations and then call your original hotel as soon as possible to cancel.\par \par \uc2 In the case of past strikes, Dr. Hanson said, ''Individual hotels have some discretion, but the hotel company policy was: Whatever our cancellation policy is, is the policy we will maintain during the strike. So at the very least, that is what the regular, average traveler should keep in mind and plan around,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 The cancellation policy at \uc2 Hilton\uc2 , \uc2 Hyatt\uc2 , \uc2 Marriott\uc2  and \uc2 Omni\uc2  hotels generally depends on your specific rate and dates of your stay, so you'll need to look up your reservation on their app or website.\par \par \uc2 Will my hotel stay open during the strike?\par \par \uc2 In general, hotels say they are prepared to operate without disruption.\par \par \uc2 Representatives for \uc2 Hyatt\uc2 , \uc2 Hilton\uc2  and \uc2 Omni\uc2  all said that they had contingency plans to minimize any strikes' impact on hotel operations. \uc2 Marriott\uc2  did not respond to request for comment.\par \par \uc2 While the chains would not reveal details of those plans, it is likely that they would use nonunion employees, Dr. Hanson said. They might also, for example, substitute a grab-and-go buffet for table service. And outside contractors may be hired to perform services that hotel staff would typically perform.\par \par \uc2 During the most recent major hotel strike, last summer in Los Angeles, thousands of hotel workers walked off the job just as travelers were gearing up for the Fourth of July. That first strike was resolved after three days. Sporadic walkouts at other properties throughout the summer affected housekeeping services for some guests and caused disruptions after event organizers canceled events.\par \par \uc2 Follow New York Times Travel on \uc2 Instagram\uc2  and sign up for our weekly Travel Dispatch newsletter to get expert tips on traveling smarter and inspiration for your next vacation. Dreaming up a future getaway or just armchair traveling? Check out our 52 Places to Go in 2024 .\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page B4. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240902ek920002v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
