\par\par \uc2 GUEST ESSAY\par \uc2 Sunday Review Desk; SECTSR\par \b \uc2 Workers Deserve So Much More Than America's Unions\b0\par\par\uc2 By Oren Cass \par \uc2 1,339 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 September 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 5\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 On the first night of the Democratic National Convention, six labor leaders took the stage to endorse the party's nominee. ''\uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  will keep fighting for workers,'' promised the general president of the \uc2 Laborers' International Union of North America\uc2 . The Republicans' vision, said the president of the A.F.L.-C.I.O., is ''a worker's nightmare.''\par \par \uc2 Certainly, they are right about who will fight for the labor leaders. The Democrats' Protecting the Right to Organize legislation would help them add more members to their unions. But the casual conflation of their own interests with those of workers misunderstands what workers want and what pro-worker policy can look like. If the goal is to place workers on a level playing field with employers so that they can advocate their own interests effectively and share fully in the prosperity they help to create, then getting more workers into those unions is not the only way. It's not even the best way.\par \par \uc2 A more effective strategy is to ensure a tight labor market by constraining who employers can hire -- in particular, by making it harder to offshore production to other countries or bring workers from other countries into the United States. Employers fight against such constraint tooth and nail because it forces them to improve the quality of the jobs they offer, to retain workers and draw people off the sidelines to fill open positions, and to invest in boosting productivity.\par \par \uc2 A recent analysis by the \uc2 Federal Reserve Bank of St. Louis\uc2  illustrates this effect. Firms lamenting labor shortages and hiring challenges for routine manual tasks tend to invest more and achieve larger productivity gains. Senator JD Vance has articulated the point well, warning that support for the free movement of labor across national borders ''decimates the bargaining power of workers'' by offering employers less expensive alternatives, while constraining access to inexpensive labor is ''a tech-forward argument. If you can't constantly do the same things with cheaper and cheaper labor, your economy is forced to innovate.''\par \par \uc2 Nor is America's existing union model, codified by the Depression-era National Labor Relations Act, the only form a vibrant labor movement might take. In most of Europe, for instance, unions operate at the industry level rather than having to fight for recognition company by company. Within individual firms, rather than face off across a negotiating table, labor and management are more likely to establish joint ''works councils,'' through which the sides collaborate on solving workplace problems and improving operations. Under the N.L.R.A., such cooperative arrangements are significantly restricted, and unions want to keep it that way, even though workers repeatedly say they would prefer worker organizations ''run jointly by employees and management.''\par \par \uc2 The idea that only the Democrats were a pro-worker party became conventional wisdom in the era between the \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  presidencies. The Republican Party regarded consumers' living standards as the ultimate measure of economic health, and focused on raising them by creating the most business-friendly conditions for investment and growth.\par \par \uc2 ''The goal of the economic system,'' argued \uc2 Glenn Hubbard\uc2 , who designed the \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  tax cuts and served as \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2 's principal economist in the 2012 presidential race, is ''optimizing consumption -- producing goods and services as cheaply as possible.'' In this vision, labor is just another input like oil or lumber: the more available, and the lower the cost, the better.\par \par \uc2 In a debate I had last year with \uc2 Stephen Moore\uc2 , the co-founder of the Club for Growth and a former member of The Wall Street Journal's editorial board, Mr. Moore provided a quintessential illustration of the mind-set. The subject of the debate was immigration and wages, yet his nearly 10-minute opening statement didn't mention wages once, just economic growth. If ''cheap labor leads to a booming stock market,'' he later said, ''that benefits everyone.''\par \par \uc2 An emerging generation of conservative leaders does not accept the old orthodoxy and is working to transform the G.O.P. Senator \uc2 Josh Hawley\uc2 , for instance, was writing as early as 2012 about the need for conservatives to ''use wise policy to nudge it toward greater inclusion of disadvantaged workers.'' In 2019, Senator \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  delivered a watershed speech at the \uc2 Catholic University of America\uc2  on ''common-good capitalism,'' in which he lamented, ''we have neglected the rights of workers to share in the benefits they create for their employer.'' Rejecting the idea that the market's highest purpose is to enable maximum consumption at low, low prices, he argued, ''the most impactful benefit the market can provide our people, our society and the nation at large is the creation and availability of dignified work.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Vance gave the keynote address at the American Conservative's annual gala in 2019, ''Towards a Pro-Worker, Pro-Family Conservatism.'' In describing his conversion to Catholicism, Mr. Vance has emphasized foremost his belief that ''our society is more than the sum of its economic statistics. If people die sooner in the midst of historic levels of consumption, then perhaps our focus on consumption is misguided.'' At last month's Republican convention, he declared, ''We're done, ladies and gentlemen, catering to Wall Street. We'll commit to the working man.''\par \par \uc2 Of course, conservatives and progressives will approach this goal very differently. Conservatives are skeptical of a labor movement that has gone all-in on progressive politics. They don't believe that regulators in Washington can simply mandate that employers provide better jobs. They have much greater faith in market competition. If we get the broad rules right, they believe, then creating good jobs for American workers will become an indispensable element of building a successful business.\par \par \uc2 Thus, in 2023, Mr. Vance joined Senators \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2  and Tom Cotton in sponsoring the Higher Wages for American Workers Act, which would gradually raise the federal minimum wage by more than 50 percent, to $11 per hour. It would also create a tighter labor market by mandating that employers use the E-Verify system to confirm that jobs go to workers who are legally eligible for employment. Not a single Democrat has supported the bill. Their opposition to enforcing immigration law trumps policy that would plainly benefit American workers.\par \par \uc2 Senator Rubio and Representative Jim Banks have proposed the Teamwork for Employees and Managers (TEAM) Act of 2022, which would open a new path to employee representation via a works councils model. Under that legislation, a company creating a works council would also have to allot a seat (albeit a nonvoting one) on the corporate board to a worker representative. Again, Democrats are opposed, this time because established labor unions object to the idea of creating an alternative that they see as watered-down, ineffective representation, never mind that workers actually want it.\par \par \uc2 Is it more pro-worker to support the Republicans' combination of a minimum-wage increase and mandatory E-Verify or the Democrats' combination of union-friendly measures proposed in the Protecting the Right to Organize legislation? Is the pro-labor position to support a new form of representation that workers say they want, or to give them only an all-or-nothing choice of a union? The trend toward both parties earnestly adopting those labels is a development to celebrate, not resist or deny. There will be no shortage of fights still to have over whose ideas can deliver.\par \par \uc2 Oren Cass is the chief economist at American Compass, a conservative economic think tank, and writes the newsletter Understanding America.\par \par \uc2 The Times is committed to publishing a diversity of letters to the editor. We'd like to hear what you think about this or any of our articles. Here are some tips . And here's our email: letters@nytimes.com .\par \par \uc2 Follow the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  Opinion section on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  , \uc2 Instagram\uc2  , TikTok , WhatsApp , X and Threads .\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page SR5. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240901ek9100064\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
