\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Kroger\uc2 's $25 Billion Takeover of \uc2 Albertsons\uc2  Is Bad for Shoppers, F.T.C. Tells Court\b0\par\par\uc2 By Danielle Kaye \par \uc2 1,178 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 August 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Federal Trade Commission\uc2 , which is trying to block \uc2 Kroger\uc2 's plan to acquire \uc2 Albertsons\uc2 , said in court that the merger of grocery giants would also hurt workers' pay and benefits.\par \par \uc2 A trial that could determine whether the two largest supermarket chains in the United States can merge opened in Portland, Ore., on Monday, pitting the grocery giant \uc2 Kroger\uc2  against regulators who argue that its takeover of \uc2 Albertsons\uc2  would eliminate competition at the expense of consumers and workers.\par \par \uc2 Before Judge Adrienne Nelson of U.S. District Court, the \uc2 Federal Trade Commission\uc2  and the supermarket chains laid out their arguments in court for the first time, as union representatives and workers protested the deal on the courthouse steps. Less competition, the agency's lawyers said, would give \uc2 Kroger\uc2  more leverage to raise prices on millions of consumers.\par \par \uc2 The highly anticipated proceedings, set to last three weeks, come as high food prices have become a critical focus in the presidential race. Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , the Democratic presidential nominee, has backed a federal ban on price-gouging in the food and grocery industries to combat high grocery costs.\par \par \uc2 Kroger\uc2  and \uc2 Albertsons\uc2  defended the $24.6 billion deal, which would be the biggest supermarket merger in U.S. history, saying it would bolster their leverage with suppliers and improve competition against major retailers like \uc2 Costco\uc2 , \uc2 Amazon\uc2  and \uc2 Walmart\uc2 . But the F.T.C. -- backed by a chorus of unions, consumer advocates, politicians and independent grocery chains -- reiterated its position that the merger would probably result in higher prices for groceries and worse conditions for workers.\par \par \uc2 The deal ''would eliminate the competition that shoppers and workers depend on in one fell swoop,'' Susan Musser, the F.T.C.'s chief trial counsel, said in her opening statement. ''This lawsuit is part of an effort aimed at helping Americans feed their families.''\par \par \uc2 In bringing the case, the F.T.C. has been joined by the attorneys general of eight states, including California and Illinois, as well as the District of Columbia. It's part of a regulatory push under the Biden administration to rein in corporate consolidation in an array of industries, including airlines, Big Tech, book publishing and pharmaceuticals.\par \par \uc2 The grocery industry has seen waves of consolidation since the 1990s, and the landscape is now dominated by four players: \uc2 Walmart\uc2 , \uc2 Kroger\uc2 , \uc2 Costco\uc2  and \uc2 Albertsons\uc2 . If allowed to combine, \uc2 Kroger\uc2  and \uc2 Albertsons\uc2  would account for around 13 percent of U.S. grocery sales.\par \par \uc2 The F.T.C. filed its antitrust lawsuit in February, about a year and a half after the grocery giants announced their merger. It's the first challenge to the deal to go to court; hearings for lawsuits filed by the \uc2 Washington State\uc2  and Colorado attorneys general are scheduled for the coming weeks.\par \par \uc2 The hearing in federal court is over a preliminary injunction. If Judge Nelson rules in the F.T.C.'s favor, the merger would be blocked pending the outcome of the agency's separate internal proceeding over the deal, to be held before an administrative law judge. \uc2 Kroger\uc2  sued the F.T.C. last week, calling its judges unconstitutional in an attempt to prevent the in-house tribunal from moving forward.\par \par \uc2 A preliminary injunction from a federal judge would most likely influence the outcome of the F.T.C.'s internal proceeding. The hearing now underway is largely being treated as a mini trial on the merits.\par \par \uc2 The F.T.C. is arguing that the merger would decrease price competition, leading to higher costs for shoppers. But it also contends that the deal would erode the bargaining power of unions and harm not just consumers but workers as well.\par \par \uc2 At a news conference on the courthouse steps Monday morning, \uc2 Kim Cordova\uc2 , president of the Colorado and Wyoming chapter of the \uc2 United Food and Commercial Workers International Union\uc2 , said the merger would reduce union power at the bargaining table, resulting in lower wages and worse benefits. The union represents most in-store employees at both companies.\par \par \uc2 ''This kind of power is a fundamental right for workers,'' Ms. Cordova said.\par \par \uc2 The merger would create a $200 billion company, with about 5,000 stores in 48 states and the District of Columbia. \uc2 Kroger\uc2 , based in Cincinnati, operates nearly 2,800 stores across the United States under banners that include Ralphs, Dillons and \uc2 Harris Teeter\uc2 . \uc2 Albertsons\uc2 , based in Boise, Idaho, runs more than 2,200 supermarkets under names like \uc2 Safeway\uc2  and Vons.\par \par \uc2 The F.T.C. faces a critical legal hurdle in showing the merger would reduce competition: Its lawyers need to convince Judge Nelson that the traditional supermarket industry, specifically, is the appropriate scope of analysis for assessing harm to competition -- and that competition in the grocery industry happens at the local level.\par \par \uc2 Matthew Wolf, a lawyer for \uc2 Kroger\uc2 , said in his opening statement that the broader landscape of retailers that have made inroads into selling groceries -- including online retailers like \uc2 Amazon\uc2  and bulk retailers like \uc2 Costco\uc2  -- should be included in the assessment, an approach that would make it easier for the companies to argue that the merger would not give them undue dominance.\par \par \uc2 Competition with these bigger players is at the heart of \uc2 Kroger\uc2 's justification for the deal. Currently, \uc2 Walmart\uc2  alone accounts for around 22 percent of U.S. grocery sales. Lawyers for \uc2 Kroger\uc2  and \uc2 Albertsons\uc2  portrayed the deal as a lifeline for traditional supermarkets strained by the increasing dominance of nontraditional rivals.\par \par \uc2 ''Supermarkets are losing this food fight'' to \uc2 Walmart\uc2 , \uc2 Costco\uc2  and \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , Mr. Wolf said.\par \par \uc2 The merger would lead to lower prices for shoppers at \uc2 Albertsons\uc2 , where prices tend to be higher than at \uc2 Kroger\uc2  stores, Mr. Wolf said. \uc2 Kroger\uc2  has said it would lower grocery prices by $1 billion if the merger were to close.\par \par \uc2 The two chains have significant overlap in some markets, including Los Angeles, Seattle and Chicago. \uc2 Kroger\uc2  has taken steps to try to allay regulators' concerns about market concentration in such areas. The company said it would sell 579 \uc2 Kroger\uc2  and \uc2 Albertsons\uc2  stores to a third company, called C&S Wholesale Grocers.\par \par \uc2 But the F.T.C. came out strongly against the proposal.\par \par \uc2 ''Hundreds of markets are unremedied by the divestiture,'' said Laura Hall, the F.T.C.'s senior trial counsel. ''C&S is unlikely to be an effective competitor for a litany of reasons.''\par \par \uc2 Looming over the proposal is the failed outcome of a similar deal in 2015, when \uc2 Albertsons\uc2  sold dozens of stores to a third company in order to acquire its rival \uc2 Safeway\uc2 . That company, Haggen, ultimately went bankrupt, closing some stores and selling many back to \uc2 Albertsons\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The F.T.C. is expected to call expert witnesses to testify about the store sales, as well as competition in the grocery industry, before the hearing is scheduled to wrap up on Sept. 13. The chief executives of \uc2 Kroger\uc2  and \uc2 Albertsons\uc2  are expected to take the stand next week.\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page B4. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240827ek8r00047\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
