\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 F.T.C. Suit Brings Up Antitrust And Labor\b0\par\par\uc2 By Danielle Kaye \par \uc2 1,922 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 August 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 As \uc2 Kroger\uc2  seeks to acquire \uc2 Albertsons\uc2 , federal regulators argue that the biggest supermarket combination in history will hurt not only consumers, but workers as well.\par \par \uc2 Back in 2015, Leonard De Monte was feeling settled. At 31, he had health insurance and was making a union wage at the Vons grocery store in Woodland Hills, Calif., where he had worked for more than a decade. A familiar face in the bakery section, he knew dozens of frequent shoppers' orders by heart.\par \par \uc2 Then came a corporate merger: \uc2 Albertsons\uc2 , Vons's parent company, acquired its rival \uc2 Safeway\uc2 . Mr. De Monte's store was sold to a third chain as part of the deal, and within months of the change, the store's new owner declared bankruptcy. Mr. De Monte found himself out of work.\par \par \uc2 Former customers vouched for him, and he found a new job at a local Pavilions, part of another grocery chain owned by \uc2 Albertsons\uc2 . But he had lost his seniority and was demoted to minimum wage.\par \par \uc2 ''All my hard work was flushed down the toilet,'' Mr. De Monte said.\par \par \uc2 Now, nearly 10 years older and having finally worked his way up to a wage of nearly $27 per hour, he's experiencing d\u233\'00\'E9j\u224\'00\'E0 vu: \uc2 Albertsons\uc2  is trying to merge with \uc2 Kroger\uc2  in a $24.6 billion deal that will be the biggest grocery combination in history if it goes through. The two chains have agreed to sell 579 stores -- out of about 5,000 -- to a third company in an effort to satisfy antitrust regulators. The Pavilions where Mr. De Monte works is on that list.\par \par \uc2 Mergers often create anxiety for workers who stand to lose jobs or benefits when companies combine. The \uc2 United Food and Commercial Workers International Union\uc2 , or U.F.C.W., which represents most in-store workers at \uc2 Kroger\uc2  and \uc2 Albertsons\uc2 , has spoken out against the proposed deal, though it doesn't have much ability to stop it.\par \par \uc2 But the union does have a powerful ally: the \uc2 Federal Trade Commission\uc2 . The agency sued to block the combination, and a trial that will decide whether the two chains can join forces is scheduled to start in federal court in Oregon on Monday.\par \par \uc2 The F.T.C. is making arguments typical of antitrust enforcers in recent decades: The merger will decrease competition, leading to higher prices for consumers.\par \par \uc2 But within its legal complaint is another claim, one that has surprised some antitrust experts because of its novelty. The combination of the nation's two biggest supermarket chains, the F.T.C. argues, would erode the bargaining power of unions and harm not just consumers, but workers as well.\par \par \uc2 Starting in the late 1970s, after a period of robust antitrust enforcement, regulators eased up on challenging corporate mergers. Regulators under the Biden administration, however, have made cracking down on corporate concentration a priority. And for the first time, merger guidelines updated last year by the F.T.C. and the Justice Department explicitly outline the agencies' emphasis on how corporate mergers could reduce competition for workers and result in lower wages or worse benefits.\par \par \uc2 ''Recognizing that there's a web of intersecting harm that can happen is an extension, in my mind, of the underlying principles of antitrust enforcement,'' said Christine Bartholomew, a professor at the University at Buffalo School of Law who teaches antitrust. ''The pendulum is swinging back to recognize the broader types of harm from anticompetitive conduct.''\par \par \uc2 The attorneys general of Colorado and Washington State, who have separately sued to block the supermarket deal, also centered workers in their complaints.\par \par \uc2 The grocery industry has seen waves of consolidation since the 1990s. Now just four companies -- \uc2 Walmart\uc2 , \uc2 Kroger\uc2 , \uc2 Costco\uc2  and \uc2 Albertsons\uc2  -- account for about half of all grocery sales.\par \par \uc2 Kroger\uc2  and \uc2 Albertsons\uc2  collectively employ about 700,000 people. The new corporation would operate under the \uc2 Kroger\uc2  name, and a \uc2 Kroger\uc2  spokeswoman said all frontline workers would keep their jobs and existing union contracts. But Mr. De Monte is not convinced that his job and benefits would be guaranteed, or that the chain buying his store would keep it open.\par \par \uc2 His wounds from the last merger are still fresh.\par \par \uc2 A Regulatory Shift\par \par \uc2 The F.T.C.'s position today looks very different from the one it took in 2015. Back then, the regulator approved the merger of \uc2 Albertsons\uc2  and \uc2 Safeway\uc2 , satisfied that the 146 stores eventually sold to a third party -- Haggen -- would prevent dominance by a single supermarket chain in certain markets.\par \par \uc2 The U.F.C.W. did not strongly object to that merger or to the sale of stores, either, something the union came to regret once Haggen filed for bankruptcy and thousands of workers lost their jobs.\par \par \uc2 This time around, \uc2 Kroger\uc2  and \uc2 Albertsons\uc2  have proposed a similar solution to gain antitrust approval: selling 579 stores -- along the West Coast and in Colorado, Arizona, Illinois and a handful of other states -- to a company called C&S Wholesale Grocers. But the F.T.C. is not convinced that separating out about a tenth of the stores would effectively maintain competition or mitigate the harm to workers and consumers.\par \par \uc2 Although only about 13 percent of grocery store workers are unionized, most of the workers at \uc2 Kroger\uc2  and \uc2 Albertsons\uc2  are represented by the U.F.C.W.\par \par \uc2 ''I have great health benefits because I've been with the company so long,'' Mr. De Monte said, adding that he needs regular checkups because of a past cancer diagnosis. ''If I lose my health benefits, I would have to pay out of pocket.''\par \par \uc2 The U.F.C.W. is concerned that the combined strength of \uc2 Kroger\uc2  and \uc2 Albertsons\uc2  would intensify a power imbalance with the union. John Marshall, a financial analyst for U.F.C.W. chapters in California and Washington State, said that, individually, both chains had been aggressive at the bargaining table. In 2003, they each demanded concessions from the U.F.C.W., including the introduction of a two-tiered pay structure. Despite setbacks, unionized workers at the companies have retained health and retirement benefits that their counterparts at nonunion rivals like \uc2 Walmart\uc2  lack.\par \par \uc2 Kroger\uc2  has said it needs to merge with \uc2 Albertsons\uc2  to compete against \uc2 Walmart\uc2  and \uc2 Amazon\uc2 . \uc2 Walmart\uc2  employs two million people and has been accused of illegal union busting, allegations the company has denied. A \uc2 Kroger\uc2  spokeswoman said nonunion rivals would become ''even more powerful and unaccountable'' if the merger was blocked.\par \par \uc2 The F.T.C., however, argues that a combined \uc2 Kroger\uc2  and \uc2 Albertsons\uc2  would erode unions' ability to negotiate better pay and benefits in bargaining talks.\par \par \uc2 ''The unions that represent grocery workers leverage the fact that \uc2 Kroger\uc2  and \uc2 Albertsons\uc2  are separate companies competing for customers and workers to negotiate better terms of employment for union grocery workers,'' the F.T.C. complaint reads. The deal would ''eliminate that competition'' and lead to lower wages, worse benefits and weaker worker protections. An agency representative declined to provide additional comment beyond the legal complaint.\par \par \uc2 Eric Posner, a professor at the University of Chicago Law School who focuses on antitrust, noted that a more dominant \uc2 Kroger\uc2  would chip away at unions' ability to use strikes as a bargaining tool.\par \par \uc2 ''If the worker can find an equally good job elsewhere, then the workers can stay on strike longer, and that means the employer will have to give and make concessions,'' Mr. Posner said.\par \par \uc2 He said he was not aware of any other antitrust cases that limited the scope of harm to unionized workers. And regulators have raised labor-related concerns in only one other case that has gone to court, Mr. Posner added. In that 2022 suit, the Justice Department successfully blocked a merger of book publishers, focusing on authors as workers who stood to be harmed by the deal.\par \par \uc2 Closings and Layoffs Feared\par \par \uc2 On top of weakened bargaining power, workers -- especially those who experienced the fallout from \uc2 Albertsons\uc2 's takeover of \uc2 Safeway\uc2  a decade ago -- are concerned about potential store closings and layoffs.\par \par \uc2 Michael Lawing, a meat manager at an \uc2 Albertsons\uc2  in the Seattle area, has been an employee of the company on and off since 1987. He said he and all his colleagues lost their jobs when their store switched over to Haggen ownership in 2015.\par \par \uc2 ''I lost all my seniority as far as vacation time, as far as health benefits,'' Mr. Lawing said. ''I had to restart from the beginning.''\par \par \uc2 Kroger\uc2  has portrayed C&S, which has signed up to buy the 579 stores that would be shed under the merger, as a pro-union operator. Lauren La Bruno, a C&S spokeswoman, said the company would recognize the union work force and honor all collective bargaining agreements.\par \par \uc2 But Mr. Marshall of the U.F.C.W. said that at two meetings in January, C&S representatives had refused to promise to negotiate new collective bargaining agreements with the union once the current contracts expired. Contracts covering more than 100,000 \uc2 Kroger\uc2  and \uc2 Albertsons\uc2  workers, mostly on the West Coast, are set to run out next year, he said. Ms. La Bruno did not respond to a request for comment on those meetings.\par \par \uc2 C&S is primarily in the wholesale grocery supply business and currently operates just 23 supermarkets nationwide, according to the F.T.C. While Ms. La Bruno said the company had enough financial strength and experience in food retailing to operate hundreds more stores, antitrust experts and regulators say another Haggen-style collapse is likely if the deal goes through. They argue that C&S doesn't appear to be equipped to efficiently operate hundreds of supermarkets.\par \par \uc2 ''This company might just shut down the stores after buying them,'' Mr. Posner said.\par \par \uc2 Yasmin Ashur, who has been an \uc2 Albertsons\uc2  employee for nearly 25 years, works as a cashier at one of the company's stores in Port Orchard, Wash., which is set to be sold to C&S. Her pension and health insurance are top of mind as she thinks about what will happen after the current U.F.C.W. contract expires.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Ashur earns $26 an hour, about $10 above the minimum wage in her state. She said there were other places she could look for work, if need be -- maybe at a nursing home or a discount retailer like \uc2 Big Lots\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''But then again, you're starting from scratch,'' Ms. Ashur said. ''Nobody will hire you for whatever I was making, and I'm going to have to start from the bottom.''\par \par \uc2 Pavilions in West Hills, Calif., part of another grocery chain owned by Albertsons. The United Food and Commercial Workers International Union has spoken out against a proposed deal.; Leonard De Monte, a Pavillions' employee who found himself out of work after a previous grocery store merger and had to start from scratch. After having finally worked his way up to a wage of nearly $27 per hour, he is anxious another merger will cost him his job and benefits again. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY MARK ABRAMSON FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES); Yasmin Ashur, who has been an Albertsons' employee for nearly 25 years in Port Orchard, Wash., and worked her way up to earning about $10 more than the minimum wage in her state. The store is set to be sold to C&S and Ms. Ashur is concerned about how the sale will affect her. (PHOTOGRAPH BY JOVELLE TAMAYO FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (B2) This article appeared in print on page B1, B2. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240826ek8q0004b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
