\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Geopolitics Setting Back Businesses\b0\par\par\uc2 By Peter S. Goodman \par \uc2 1,482 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 August 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Alarmed by the prospect of increased tariffs on Chinese goods if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is elected, some American companies are stockpiling parts and delaying expansions.\par \par \uc2 If not for the trade war between the world's two largest economies, Easy Signs would now be hiring dozens of workers at its factory in Allentown, Pa. It would be readying plans to build a second plant somewhere out West -- Salt Lake City was a contender -- generating another 100 jobs.\par \par \uc2 Based in Australia, Easy Signs manufactures banners and marketing installations for corporate events, using huge printers to press logos and slogans onto rolls of cloth. Its American business has been growing 70 percent a year. Still, the company is putting off an expansion.\par \par \uc2 Its cloth signs are displayed on aluminum stands made in China. Those products are now subject to a series of tariffs reaching as high as 365 percent under a policy set in motion by former President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  and continued by the Biden administration in the name of protecting American industry from Chinese government subsidies.\par \par \uc2 The costs of imported components could increase further should Mr. Trump win November's presidential election and follow through on his threat to add a tariff of 60 percent or more on all Chinese goods, and 10 percent to all imports.\par \par \uc2 ''That's definitely a scary concept,'' said Andy Fryer, co-founder of Easy Signs. ''The whole feasibility goes out the window.''\par \par \uc2 In interviews with two dozen American manufacturers, retailers and shipping agents, many said they were holding off on investments and expansion given the uncertainty over tariffs on imported products and parts -- especially on those shipped from China. If Mr. Trump wins the election, the potential for further disruption is great. If Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  wins, many of the people said, they expect trade hostilities with China to continue, threatening increased costs for components used by American businesses.\par \par \uc2 ''Companies have kind of decided that, regardless of what government is coming into power, tariffs on China are going to continue to get higher,'' said Mandeep Singh, whose business, Via Indigos in Cincinnati, connects American factories with suppliers in India.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Singh was recently contacted by a company that makes air purifiers and was eager to shift production from China to India. ''They want us to take care of the entire product range,'' he said. ''They would like to find a solution earlier than later.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump began imposing broad tariffs on imports from China in 2018. President Biden has extended that policy, adding tariffs to new categories of Chinese goods including electric vehicles and solar cells.\par \par \uc2 Both administrations said the levies were intended to spur the return of factory jobs to the United States. But even as tariffs on Chinese imports have pressured manufacturers and retailers to reduce dependence on China, they have typically caused production to shift to other low-wage countries like Mexico, Vietnam and India.\par \par \uc2 Some businesses are stockpiling inventory by increasing imports, according to a recent analysis by \uc2 Project44\uc2 , a company that provides technology used by businesses to manage their supply chains. In June and July -- the peak time for retailers amassing inventory for the holiday season -- the volume of ocean shipments reaching the United States from China increased more than 4 percent from the previous year, according to the report.\par \par \uc2 Other companies are riding out the current confusion, having grown accustomed to unrelenting chaos in the supply chain. The pandemic upended global manufacturing and shipping, yielding floating traffic jams off ports and product shortages.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Suez Canal\uc2  has been rendered nearly off limits by strikes on vessels by Houthi rebels in Yemen acting in solidarity with Palestinians in Gaza, sending shipping prices skyward. The Panama Canal has limited traffic in the face of a drought. Rail workers in Canada are threatening to strike along with dockworkers at ports on the East Coast and the Gulf of Mexico.\par \par \uc2 ''My approach is wait and see, and then move as fast as we can if it does happen,'' said Sam Shackleton, whose Boston e-commerce business, Cross Path Capital, relies on Chinese factories to produce home d\u233\'00\'E9cor and garden design products that it sells on \uc2 Amazon\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Previous tariffs have increased the costs of some of his products by more than 30 percent. He has raised prices to pass on roughly half that cost. His suppliers in China have reduced prices on some items, sharing the burden of the tariffs, he added.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Shackleton has considered finding suppliers in other countries but has stuck with China because it presents an unbeatable combination of high quality and low prices, he said.\par \par \uc2 ''We are busy with a lot of other aspects of running the business,'' he said. ''Moving around pieces on a board falls down the priority list even if we could argue that it's existential.''\par \par \uc2 Many importers are placing faith in the assumption that Mr. Trump would stop short of imposing 60 percent tariffs because that would sow chaos in the economy, menace the stock market and lift prices to uncomfortable levels.\par \par \uc2 ''Nobody I see is planning for it,'' said Sara Dandan, founder of FourOneOne, a Chicago business that books shipping containers for importers. ''It comes down to an American consumer thing. We're used to being able to go to \uc2 Walmart\uc2  and buy a $2 spatula.''\par \par \uc2 But Mr. Trump has time and again breached traditional norms. Eight years ago, trade experts scoffed at his vows to add across-the-board tariffs to Chinese imports.\par \par \uc2 With such false reassurances as a guide, some companies are concerned enough by the tariff talk to make contingency plans.\par \par \uc2 ''You have to take it seriously,'' said Chris Taylor, chief executive of GridStor, a company in Portland, Ore., that develops energy storage projects. ''I think you would ignore that at your peril.''\par \par \uc2 GridStor has been placing early orders for batteries for a project planned for the Houston area. Mr. Taylor has been urging his suppliers to move faster to set up factory operations in the United States or nearby to limit dependence on Asia. ''In every meeting, that conversation comes up,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Larger companies are hanging on to extra inventory of parts and finished goods, accepting higher costs for warehousing and insurance as a hedge against future tariff increases, said Ara Ohanian, chief executive of Netstock, which designs software used by companies to manage their supply chains. Some of his customers have been shifting orders to Mexico to avoid tariffs on Chinese imports.\par \par \uc2 But Chinese companies have themselves set up factories in Mexico, using the North American trade agreement to gain duty free access to the United States. Some American importers assume that a second Trump term would halt that arrangement. Auto companies are especially concerned, given that Mexico has become a major supplier of parts for automotive plants in the United States.\par \par \uc2 ''Everybody is getting skittish,'' said Ross George, a supply chain consultant focused on the auto industry. ''They say: 'We know we are buying from a Chinese company, but it says, ''Made in Mexico.'' It's not quite as clear cut as we'd like.'''\par \par \uc2 The solar industry appears especially vulnerable to an intensifying trade war given its heavy dependence on imported cells from Southeast Asia. Chinese companies have in recent years set up plants in Malaysia, Thailand and Vietnam to avoid American duties on exports from China. That has prompted fresh tariffs on exports from those countries, a trend expected to broaden if Mr. Trump returns to office.\par \par \uc2 Even the expectation of further trade impediments risks slowing the emergence of solar energy, said \uc2 Mike Hall\uc2 , the chief executive of Anza, a company that matches buyers and sellers of solar energy gear. ''It makes things more expensive and creates uncertainty and fear,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 In Pennsylvania, Easy Signs recently received two shipping containers full of aluminum stands at the Port of Philadelphia. Rather than pay the extra duties, it declined to accept them, instead paying extra shipping costs to return the cargo back to its warehouse in Australia.\par \par \uc2 But the company is consoled by one key area of growth. ''We're going to do a lot of election signs,'' said Amanda Strait, the factory's operations manager.\par \par \uc2 Easy Signs' business in the United States manufacturing banners and marketing installations for corporate events is growing, but the company, based in Australia, is holding off on expansion. Some of its products come from China. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY AARON RICHTER FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES); Some imports from China are subject to tariffs reaching as high as 365 percent under a policy set in motion by former President Donald J. Trump and continued by the Biden administration in the name of protecting American industry. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY AARON RICHTER FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES; HAIYUN JIANG FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (B4) This article appeared in print on page B1, B4. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240820ek8k0003p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
