\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 M.T.A. Ordered to Improve Track Safety After a Transit Worker Is Killed\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ana Ley \par \uc2 908 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 August 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 14\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Federal Transit Administration\uc2  released its audit nine months after a worker was hit by a train. If the M.T.A. does not comply, it could lose funding.\par \par \uc2 Federal transportation officials said on Wednesday that the death of a transit worker who was hit by a train in November, as well as a rising number of ''near-miss'' incidents on train tracks last year, reflected unsafe conditions and practices that were putting transit employees at growing risk.\par \par \uc2 In an audit, the \uc2 Federal Transit Administration\uc2  counted 38 events in which track workers were involved in close calls in 2023. That tally was up from 24 incidents in 2022 and 23 in 2021.\par \par \uc2 The majority of the dangerous events were caused by the failure of transit employees to ''comply with key safety rules and established procedures,'' according to the F.T.A.\par \par \uc2 Half of the ''near-miss'' incidents last year involved one or more transit workers who failed to follow proper procedures while flagging -- the job of notifying trains when workers are on the tracks. Other incidents involved factors such as improper communication and radio use, inadequate protection and train operator inattention. A ''near-miss'' incident is defined as an event in which death or injury is narrowly avoided and typically happens when a worker is struck by a train, steps on the third rail or slips and falls on the tracks.\par \par \uc2 ''The volume of close calls is pretty worrying,'' said Jim Mathews, chief executive of the Rail Passengers Association, an advocacy group. ''If you work in and around the subway, you've had an awful lot of close calls, and eventually close calls catch up to you.''\par \par \uc2 The F.T.A., which is part of the \uc2 U.S. Department of Transportation\uc2 , drew up a list of remedies for the unsafe working conditions that included an updated safety plan from the \uc2 Metropolitan Transportation Authority\uc2 , which oversees the city's transit network.\par \par \uc2 The M.T.A. disputed the findings in a letter sent to the federal agency and said it planned to appeal.\par \par \uc2 Demetrius Crichlow, the interim president of New York City Transit, the division of the M.T.A. that manages the subway, said the authority had robust safety protocols in place protecting workers. ''We strongly dispute F.T.A.'s view that N.Y.C.T. has somehow been negligent when it comes to addressing the safety of track workers, one of our most essential priorities,'' he said in the letter.\par \par \uc2 He added that the years under comparison in the F.T.A.'s analysis included a period during the pandemic when there was little construction work in the subway system, potentially accounting for the recent increase in the number of incidents.\par \par \uc2 The audit followed a string of incidents in the transit network that incensed workers and unnerved riders. In November, a track worker was struck by a D train while flagging. In January, there were two separate and unrelated train derailments within a week. (The first derailment, which occurred when two trains collided, was determined to have most likely been caused by human error.) Then in June, a train struck and critically injured a transit worker near the Hoyt-Schermerhorn Street Station.\par \par \uc2 Another federal agency, the \uc2 National Transportation Safety Board\uc2 , is carrying out separate investigations into the death of the track worker and the collision of the trains in January. Those investigations have not yet finished and could take up to two years to complete.\par \par \uc2 The transit administration said the M.T. A. had done little after dangerous incidents beyond issuing safety advisories and bulletins to workers reminding them of safety procedures. The F.T.A.'s review also found a ''clear pattern of rule violations contributing to these near-miss events.'' The federal investigators said that the repeating nature of certain violations, such as improper flagging, suggested ''systemic issues'' that required increased monitoring of the M.T.A.'s safety measures for track workers.\par \par \uc2 The transit administration has directed the M.T.A. to immediately carry out a risk assessment of its safety program for track workers and update its practices. It has also ordered the authority to set up weekly meetings to review any new incidents, among other requirements.\par \par \uc2 If the M.T.A. does not comply with the transit administration's orders, it risks losing a portion of its federal funding, among other penalties. The authority has one month to file an appeal on the findings.\par \par \uc2 Additionally on Wednesday, the transit administration said that New York State's Public Transportation Safety Board, which is charged with overseeing rail safety, had not done enough to verify the accuracy of the reports it receives from the M.T.A. The F.T.A. noted the board was holding about $8.1 million in unspent federal funds that had been designated for track safety oversight.\par \par \uc2 While the F.T.A. focused its study on transit workers, the agency also noted 228 ''near-miss'' incidents that could have resulted in an injury to a passenger or property damage last year. That number was up from 222 in 2022 and 156 in 2021.\par \par \uc2 A track inspection. Officials said an audit of the M.T.A. had shown failures to follow safety rules. (PHOTOGRAPH BY EMIL SALMAN FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A14. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240817ek8h00032\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picsc