\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Cisco\uc2  to Cut About 7% of Staff In Second Round of Layoffs\b0\par\par\uc2 By Eli Tan \par \uc2 330 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 August 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The cuts could amount to about 6,000 jobs for the San Jose, Calif., maker of computer networking technology.\par \par \uc2 Cisco Systems\uc2 , one of the largest makers of computer networking equipment, said on Wednesday that it would cut 7 percent of its work force in its second major round of layoffs this year.\par \par \uc2 The company did not say how many workers would be affected, but it reported it had 84,900 employees in July 2023, which would amount to around 6,000 jobs cut. In February, \uc2 Cisco\uc2  laid off 4,000 employees.\par \par \uc2 In an earnings report, \uc2 Cisco\uc2  posted net income of $2.16 billion in its most recent quarter, down 45 percent from a year earlier, and revenue of $13.64 billion, down 10 percent. Still, the company exceeded Wall Street expectations, and its stock was up nearly 6 percent in after-hours trading.\par \par \uc2 Like other big tech companies, \uc2 Cisco\uc2  has raced to take advantage of an industrywide shift into artificial intelligence. While \uc2 Cisco\uc2  has long been one of Silicon Valley's largest companies, and briefly was the world's most valuable publicly traded company during the dot-com boom, it has been overshadowed in recent years by companies offering cloud computing services to big corporate customers.\par \par \uc2 Still, the company, which is based in San Jose, Calif., has managed to be very profitable through the years. In March, it closed its deal to acquire \uc2 Splunk\uc2 , a cybersecurity and analytic company, for $28 billion.\par \par \uc2 In a statement on Wednesday, \uc2 Chuck Robbins\uc2 , \uc2 Cisco\uc2 's chairman and chief executive, said the company planned to further integrate \uc2 Splunk\uc2  into its products. It will also combine its networking, security and collaboration departments into one organization led by \uc2 Jeetu Patel\uc2 , Mr. Robbins said. \uc2 Jonathan Davidson\uc2 , Cisco Networking's general manager and executive vice president, will become an adviser to Mr. Robbins.\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page B3. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240816ek8g00032\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
