\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Uber\uc2 , Facing Sexual-Assault Litigation, Pushes Plan That May Curb Suits\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jessica Silver-Greenberg \par \uc2 1,538 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 August 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Uber\uc2  has spent millions trying to get a proposal on the Nevada ballot that would restrict the legal fees that bankroll many lawsuits against companies.\par \par \uc2 It has been a decades-long dream of corporate America: making it harder for aggrieved customers, workers and others to file lawsuits against companies seeking compensation for alleged injuries, mistreatment and other harms.\par \par \uc2 Now \uc2 Uber\uc2 , the giant ride-hailing company that has been a magnet for such litigation, is pitching a plan that could fulfill that fantasy, at least in one state.\par \par \uc2 The company is seeking to place a measure on Nevada's ballot that would drastically limit the amount of money that lawyers could collect when they brought successful lawsuits. If the measure passes, it will make it less attractive -- and in some cases financially impractical -- for plaintiffs' lawyers to file such suits.\par \par \uc2 The initiative is being spearheaded by Nevadans for Fair Recovery, a political action committee created this year by \uc2 Uber\uc2  and its lobbyists. The company has put $5 million into the PAC and is its only financial backer, campaign finance records show.\par \par \uc2 The PAC has presented the measure as a way to protect people from trial lawyers who are ''getting rich at the expense of plaintiffs and everyone else,'' as the group says on its website alongside a picture of someone sliding a $100 dollar bill into a suit pocket.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 Uber\uc2  is pushing the proposal at the same time that the company faces a torrent of lawsuits in Nevada and elsewhere from customers who have accused it of failing to protect them from being sexually assaulted or harassed by drivers. The suits have been consolidated in federal court, a move that is likely to make it easier for additional victims to come forward with similar claims.\par \par \uc2 The initiative has the potential to help shield \uc2 Uber\uc2  from that onslaught.\par \par \uc2 The ballot measure would prohibit lawyers in Nevada from collecting more than 20 percent of their clients' jury awards or settlements in the form of so-called contingency fees. Today, those fees can be as high as 40 percent.\par \par \uc2 Contingency fees allow lawyers to take on cases without requiring clients to pay anything out of pocket. Lawyers shoulder the costs of bringing the lawsuit in exchange for a cut of any settlement or judgment. Large payments in successful cases help cover the costs of lawsuits that do not yield any money. But businesses have long complained that high contingency fees drive up costs for the public and exist primarily to enrich lawyers.\par \par \uc2 The proposed 20 percent cap in Nevada would be the most stringent in the country. Only one other state, Oklahoma, has a broad cap on contingency fees, and that ceiling is 50 percent.\par \par \uc2 Many legal experts said a ceiling of 20 percent would make it financially difficult for many lawyers to take on complex cases against deep-pocketed companies -- and could help shield \uc2 Uber\uc2  from additional lawsuits from people who blamed the company for misconduct by its drivers.\par \par \uc2 ''This would basically be slamming the door of the courthouse on individuals who were harmed,'' said Herbert M. Kritzer, a law professor emeritus at the \uc2 University of Minnesota\uc2 , who has studied contingency fees.\par \par \uc2 Three people familiar with \uc2 Uber\uc2 's legal strategy said the company hoped the initiative would insulate \uc2 Uber\uc2  from what could be a flood of litigation related to its drivers' misconduct. One of those people said the company was also trying to make it harder for lawyers to file frivolous lawsuits against drivers for traffic accidents.\par \par \uc2 Harry Hartfield, senior manager of public affairs at \uc2 Uber\uc2  in Nevada, denied that was the intent. ''This measure does not limit \uc2 Uber\uc2 's exposure to lawsuits, period, and it's simply false to suggest otherwise,'' he said. The goal, Mr. Hartfield said, is to ensure that plaintiffs receive at least 80 percent of the proceeds of their lawsuits.\par \par \uc2 Until 2018, \uc2 Uber\uc2  forced many passengers with complaints into arbitration, a legal process that typically favors companies and allows them to keep allegations of wrongdoing out of public view. \uc2 Uber\uc2  and other tech companies abandoned that practice for sexual assault claims after intense public pressure.\par \par \uc2 The shift was part of \uc2 Uber\uc2 's broader effort to remake its image. The company's chief legal officer, Tony West, said at the time that the change would ''give sexual assault and harassment survivors control of how they pursue their claims.'' (Mr. West is preparing to go on leave from \uc2 Uber\uc2  to help his sister-in-law, Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , run for president.)\par \par \uc2 This is not the first time that \uc2 Uber\uc2  has used a ballot measure to try to change the law in its favor.\par \par \uc2 In 2020, \uc2 Uber\uc2  and other companies in the so-called gig economy spent about $200 million on a proposal in California that classified their drivers as independent contractors who were not automatically eligible for some benefits. The initiative, which succeeded, was the most expensive in the state's history.\par \par \uc2 To get the latest measure on the ballot in Nevada, organizers will have to gather 100,000 signatures from state residents and survive a legal challenge that claims the initiative's wording is misleading. Mr. Hartfield said the company expected to have those signatures in hand this month. \uc2 Uber\uc2  has said it wants the proposal to be put to voters in 2026.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Hartfield accused plaintiffs' lawyers in Nevada of lining their pockets at the expense of clients and sent \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  a photo of a personal-injury lawyer lounging on a private plane. He said \uc2 Uber\uc2  turned to the initiative process rather than trying to push a bill through the Legislature because the state government was controlled by trial lawyers.\par \par \uc2 Uber\uc2  is joining a long-running effort by tobacco, drug and other giant companies to vilify plaintiffs' lawyers as unscrupulous, greedy ''ambulance chasers,'' said Michael McCann, a professor emeritus of political science at the \uc2 University of Washington\uc2  and an author of ''Distorting the Law: Politics, Media, and the Litigation Crisis.''\par \par \uc2 The campaign started in the 1970s, Mr. McCann said, as class-action lawsuits against pharmaceutical companies and asbestos makers were gaining steam. Groups backed by corporations circulated advertisements in major newspapers, highlighting nightmarish -- and often fabricated -- cases like the woman who supposedly microwaved her cat to dry it off and then sued the microwave maker for not putting a warning on the device.\par \par \uc2 Some courts, as well as researchers from across the political spectrum, have found that contingency fees help align the interests of lawyers and their clients.\par \par \uc2 Still, \uc2 Uber\uc2 's messaging is resonating with the public, according to polls conducted by the PAC. Roughly 86 percent of respondents in Nevada said they thought the current system overcompensated lawyers and left victims with too little money.\par \par \uc2 Opponents of the measure, including a group called \uc2 Uber\uc2  Sexual Assault Survivors for Legal Accountability, filed a lawsuit in April asking a judge to block the measure from making it to the ballot, arguing that it misleads voters in violation of state law. A state judge dismissed the suit, though it is currently being appealed.\par \par \uc2 Astrid Perez, a lawyer who handles worker-compensation cases in Reno, said that she had recently been approached at an outdoor dance party by a man who asked her to sign a petition to help get the initiative on the ballot. Ms. Perez said the canvasser had told her -- incorrectly -- that lawyers in the state charged up to 80 percent in contingency fees.\par \par \uc2 The two started talking, and Ms. Perez informed him that \uc2 Uber\uc2  was behind the measure. ''He didn't know and was surprised,'' she said, ''but he told me that he'd already gotten all the signatures he needed for the day.''\par \par \uc2 So far, \uc2 Uber\uc2  has received support from only a few other business groups. The Nevada Trucking Association's chief executive, Paul Enos, said in a statement that members were ''constantly hit with frivolous lawsuits.'' The American Tort Reform Association, whose backers have included major drug and oil companies, also endorsed it.\par \par \uc2 Hundreds of passengers have filed lawsuits accusing \uc2 Uber\uc2  of failing to adequately screen and perform background checks on drivers who went on to sexually assault them.\par \par \uc2 Among the lawsuits is one brought by a woman, identified in court records by the initials K.K., who said an \uc2 Uber\uc2  driver picked her up last November from the Cosmopolitan, a swanky hotel in Las Vegas. About an hour after dropping her at home, K.K. alleged in her lawsuit, the driver entered her house while she was sleeping and raped her.\par \par \uc2 In court, \uc2 Uber\uc2  has denied wrongdoing. Mr. Hartfield said the company was committed to the safety of its riders. He said that \uc2 Uber\uc2  had stepped up its background checks of drivers -- including screening for local or federal criminal records -- and that the company had removed more than 80,000 drivers from its app as a result of that process.\par \par \uc2 Hundreds of passengers have sued Uber, accusing it of failing to properly screen drivers who went on to sexually assault them. (PHOTOGRAPH BY DOUG MILLS/THE NEW YORK TIMES) (B3) This article appeared in print on page B1, B3. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240813ek8c0000e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
