\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 The Digest\b0\par\par\uc2 By Reuters and The Associated Press \par \uc2 525 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 August 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 AUTOMOTIVE\par \par \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  Warns of Layoffs At Plant Outside of Detroit\par \par \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  is warning that it could lay off as many as 2,450 of the 3,700 union workers employed at a truck plant just outside of Detroit.\par \par \uc2 The job cuts would be at the Stellantis Warren Truck Plant, which builds an older version of the Ram 1500 pickup called the Tradesman, sold mainly to commercial businesses. The company came out with a new version of the truck in 2018, and for the 2025 model year there's a new Tradesman.\par \par \uc2 It's likely that job cuts will be lower than that because of early retirement offers that are underway, and because of seniority bumping rights, said Jodi Tinson, a \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  spokeswoman.\par \par \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  is shifting production of the new Tradesman to the Sterling Heights Assembly Plant in Sterling Heights, Mich. The company plans to lay off one shift of workers at the Warren facility, where the Jeep Wagoneer SUV is still built.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Tinson says the layoffs could begin as soon as Oct. 8. The company said indefinitely senior union employees that are let go will receive 52 weeks of supplemental unemployment benefits and 52 weeks of transition assistance..\par \par \uc2 U.S.-European automaker \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  reported that its net profits fell by half during the first six months of the year largely because of lower sales and restructuring costs. Associated Press\par \par \uc2 RETAIL\par \par \uc2 U.S. Retailers Are Rushing Imports Amid Uncertainty\par \par \uc2 Retailers are fueling a summer rush of imports to the United States this year as companies guard against a potential strike by port workers and ongoing shipping disruptions from attacks in the Red Sea ahead of a shortened holiday shopping season.\par \par \uc2 Container imports and freight rates surged in July, signaling an earlier than usual peak season for an ocean shipping industry that handles about 80 percent of global trade.\par \par \uc2 July is expected to be the peak for U.S. retailers, which account for about half of that trade, and August is expected to be almost as robust, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 Companies that import toys, home goods and consumer electronics have brought forward holiday promotions to capture customers who are shopping earlier each season. ''Retailers don't want to be caught back-footed,'' said \uc2 Jonathan Gold\uc2 , the \uc2 National Retail Federation\uc2 's vice president for supply chain and customs policy.\par \par \uc2 Many shippers expedited holiday goods orders, with some putting Christmas items on the water as early as May, said Peter Sand, chief analyst at pricing platform Xeneta.\par \par \uc2 The influx is not a result of consumer spending, which has been tethered by stubborn inflation and high interest rates, experts said. Rather, it is a precaution against a potential U.S. port strike and the late Nov. 28 date for Thanksgiving this year, squeezing the peak shopping and delivery season running to Christmas Eve.\par \par \uc2 Retailers are concerned about a possible Oct. 1 strike at seaports stretching from Maine to Texas after talks between the \uc2 International Longshoremen's Association\uc2  and the United States Maritime Alliance stalled. REUTERS\par \par \uc2 (PHOTOGRAPH BY REBECCA COOK/REUTERS) This article appeared in print on page B2. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240810ek8a0004u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
