\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECT\par \b \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  Will Lay Off Up to 2,450 at Michigan Truck Plant\b0\par\par\uc2 By Neal E. Boudette \par \uc2 675 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 August 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 The New York Times on the Web\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The move is the latest sign of trouble for the trans-Atlantic automaker, which has had sluggish North American sales and has said it needs to cut costs.\par \par \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  announced plans on Friday to lay off as many as 2,450 workers later this year at a pickup truck plant near Detroit, the latest sign of trouble for the trans-Atlantic automaker.\par \par \uc2 The layoffs are expected to begin as early as Oct. 8 at the Ram truck plant in Warren, Mich., where production will be reduced to one shift from two, the company said on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 's chief executive, \uc2 Carlos Tavares\uc2 , has said the company needs to cut costs, and he has noted that at least one North American factory was operating at an unsatisfactory level.\par \par \uc2 The company has been hit by sluggish sales in North America, where it generates most of its profits, as well as bloated costs and manufacturing inefficiencies. It reported last month that profits in the first six months of 2024 fell by nearly half to 5.6 billion euros (about $6 billion).\par \par \uc2 ''It is an understatement to say that the first-half 2024 results were disappointing and humbling,'' Mr. Tavares said on a call with analysts after the earnings report. ''This is a bump on the road that we are now fixing and that we are going to fight against to make sure that we can rebound from here, and that we fix the operational issues that we face.''\par \par \uc2 The layoffs are related to a planned transition to a new version of the Ram pickup that is just going into production at a plant in Sterling Heights, Mich. The Warren plant will continue making an older version of the truck on one shift, the company said on Friday, adding that the actual number of workers affected will probably be lower than the 2,450 noted in a report to the state of Michigan.\par \par \uc2 While some laid-off workers could be moved to other plants or return to the Warren factory at some point, the company acknowledged that some would be let go permanently. Employees who leave the company will be offered a year of unemployment benefits that supplement any assistance they receive from the state.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 United Automobile Workers union\uc2  had no immediate comment on the \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  announcement. In contract talks with the company last year, the union won the reopening of a plant in Belvidere, Ill., which the company announced in late 2022 that it planned to close, with the potential loss of 1,350 jobs.\par \par \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  was formed in the 2021 merger of \uc2 Fiat Chrysler\uc2  and the French company \uc2 Peugeot S.A.\uc2  In North America, it sells vehicles under the Chrysler, \uc2 Jeep\uc2 , Ram and Dodge brands. Its brands in Europe and other parts of the world include \uc2 Peugeot\uc2 , Citro\u235\'00\'EBn, \uc2 Fiat\uc2  and \uc2 Opel\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Mr. Tavares, 65, a Portuguese engineer who has spent most of his career in France's auto industry, has promised that the combined company would find economies of scale to deliver greater profits than its two halves had generated on their own.\par \par \uc2 Last year, it looked as if Mr. Tavares was delivering. In 2023, \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  reported a record profit of \u8364\'20\'AC18.6 billion, with much of it coming from the sale of Jeeps, Ram pickups and other trucks in the United States.\par \par \uc2 But for about the last 12 months, \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  has been producing more vehicles in North America than its customers are buying, and its inventories have grown, forcing the company to offer discounts that cut into its profit margins.\par \par \uc2 In July, Mr. Tavares said \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  was aiming to cut \u8364\'20\'AC50 million in costs in North America by the end of the year. ''We still have ahead of us a lot of cost-saving actions,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 The Stellantis plant in Warren, Mich., builds an older version of the Ram pickup truck. (PHOTOGRAPH BY Nic Antaya for The New York Times FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240810ek8a0004k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
