\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Reasons to Fear Recession, But Strengths Still Remain\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ben Casselman \par \uc2 1,214 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 August 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The economy has repeatedly defied predictions of a downturn since the pandemic recovery began. Now signs of strength contend with shakier readings.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. economy has spent three years defying expectations. It emerged from the pandemic shock more quickly and more powerfully than many experts envisioned. It proved resilient in the face of both inflation and the higher interest rates the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  used to combat it. The prospect many forecasters once considered imminent -- a recession -- looked increasingly like a false alarm.\par \par \uc2 Until now.\par \par \uc2 An unexpectedly weak jobs report on Friday -- showing slower hiring in July, and a surprising jump in unemployment -- triggered a sell-off in the stock market as investors worried that an economic downturn might be underway after all. By Monday, that decline had turned into a rout, with financial markets tumbling around the world.\par \par \uc2 Some economists said investors were overreacting to one weak but hardly disastrous report, since many indicators show the economy on fundamentally firm footing.\par \par \uc2 But they said there were also reasons to worry. Historically, increases in joblessness like the one in July -- the unemployment rate rose to 4.3 percent, the highest since 2021 -- have been a reliable indicator of a recession. And even without that precedent, there has been evidence that the labor market is weakening.\par \par \uc2 ''Even before the employment report, you were seeing some real signs of softening in the labor market,'' said \uc2 Jay Bryson\uc2 , chief economist for \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Mr. Bryson still expects a ''soft landing,'' in which inflation cools without a broad economic downturn. But the chances of a more painful outcome have risen. Other forecasters said the same: Economists at \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  over the weekend said they saw a 25 percent chance of a recession over the next year, up from 15 percent before the latest round of economic data.\par \par \uc2 More important than the predictions themselves -- which, after all, have repeatedly proved unreliable -- are the factors underlying them. The American economy no longer has the reserves of strength that helped carry it through the recent turbulence. Families no longer have a buffer of cash built up during the pandemic, or pent-up demand to spend it. Businesses no longer have a backlog of jobs to fill or shelves to restock.\par \par \uc2 None of that means a recession -- an actual contraction of the economy -- is inevitable. But it does make this economic moment more precarious than any in recent years.\par \par \uc2 Why Dour Predictions Have Been Wrong (So Far)\par \par \uc2 There is a crucial difference between the earlier, inaccurate recession forecasts and the latest set of warnings: The previous predictions were based largely on historical patterns and theoretical models. The new ones are based on actual evidence of a slowdown.\par \par \uc2 When economists began predicting a recession in 2022, inflation was high and the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  was raising interest rates aggressively to try to bring it under control. In the past, those conditions usually led to a recession: Policymakers try to curb demand just enough to bring down inflation, but end up overshooting and causing widespread layoffs.\par \par \uc2 That didn't happen this time. Consumers and businesses emerged from the pandemic with plenty of savings and relatively little debt, making them less sensitive to higher borrowing costs. At the same time, the easing of pandemic-related disruptions allowed inflationary pressures to dissipate without requiring a big drop in demand for goods and services.\par \par \uc2 ''All of those normal rules that tend to apply in both historical experience and theoretical models were very different this time around,'' said Tara Sinclair, a \uc2 George Washington University\uc2  professor who recently left a role in the Treasury Department.\par \par \uc2 As a result, even as many economists warned a recession was coming, there was never much evidence of one in the data. Hiring, wage growth and consumer spending all eased, but none collapsed. Unemployment remained near lows unseen in decades. Gross domestic product, the broadest measure of economic output, continued to grow at a healthy -- and at times robust -- pace.\par \par \uc2 Why Recession Fears Have Re-emerged\par \par \uc2 Now, though, cracks are beginning to appear. More people are falling behind on their credit card bills and car payments. Filings for unemployment benefits have begun to rise. Overall consumer spending has remained strong, but there have been signs that lower-income consumers are pulling back.\par \par \uc2 The July employment data was the clearest warning sign yet. Job growth slowed much more than expected and was concentrated in a handful of industries. The increase in the unemployment rate, the third in four months, was enough to suggest that a recession might already have begun, according to a well-known gauge known as the Sahm Rule.\par \par \uc2 Claudia Sahm\uc2 , the former Fed economist who developed the rule, said disruptions during and since the pandemic might have scrambled her namesake indicator as they had so many other once-reliable recession warning signs. But she said the underlying logic -- that even small increases in unemployment are something to worry about -- still held.\par \par \uc2 ''Given everything we know, or that we think we know, the United States is not in a recession,'' Ms. Sahm said. ''But the risk of going into a recession in, say, in the next three to six months? Those have really gone up.''\par \par \uc2 Reasons for Optimism\par \par \uc2 Still, by most measures the U.S. economy is slowing down, not stalling out.\par \par \uc2 Consumer spending, personal income and job growth -- all measures used by the \uc2 National Bureau of Economic Research\uc2  to determine when recessions begin and end -- have remained solidly positive. Gross domestic product, adjusted for inflation, grew faster in the second quarter than in the first, and is expected to show another gain in the third quarter. Other recent data have shown unexpectedly strong productivity growth and a rebounding service sector.\par \par \uc2 Better still, inflation has cooled significantly, which gives the Fed more leeway to reduce interest rates if the economy weakens further, without worrying as much that doing so will allow prices to start rising quickly again.\par \par \uc2 Jerome H. Powell\uc2 , the Fed chair, indicated in a news conference last week -- before the jobs report or the market plunge -- that the central bank could cut rates as early as its next meeting in September, something investors now view as a near certainty. Some investors expect the Fed to intervene even earlier, although careful Fed watchers consider such an emergency move unlikely.\par \par \uc2 The challenge for policymakers is that after two years of seeking to slow the economy, they are aiming to do something even more delicate: Get it to level off. And they are trying to do so with imperfect and at times contradictory data, using tools ill suited to such finely calibrated adjustments.\par \par \uc2 ''It's very hard to distinguish the difference between reaching the runway and plowing through it,'' Ms. Sinclair said.\par \par \uc2 Talmon Joseph Smith contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Talmon Joseph Smith contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 A weak jobs report on Friday triggered a stock market sell-off as investors worried about an economic decline. (PHOTOGRAPH BY GRAHAM DICKIE/THE NEW YORK TIMES) (B1); Consumer spending, personal income and job growth -- all measures used to determine when recessions begin and end -- have remained solidly positive. (PHOTOGRAPH BY GABBY JONES FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (B3) This article appeared in print on page B1, B3. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240807ek870003w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
