\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Axios C.E.O. Says 50 Layoffs Needed to Face Industry Woes\b0\par\par\uc2 By Katie Robertson \par \uc2 416 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 August 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Jim VandeHei\uc2 , the company's chief executive, said in an email that the cuts were necessary to adapt to ''changes in the media business.''\par \par \uc2 Axios announced on Tuesday that it would lay off about 50 employees, or roughly 10 percent of the company.\par \par \uc2 In a note to employees, the media outlet's chief executive, \uc2 Jim VandeHei\uc2 , attributed the cuts to ''changes in the media business'' and said that Axios needed to shift its investment to its core business areas to adapt.\par \par \uc2 ''This is the most difficult moment for media in our lifetime,'' Mr. VandeHei wrote in the email, which was obtained by \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 He said that the company would continue to hire in ''key areas'' but was facing a fragmenting of reader attention, new rivals going after its business and talent, and artificial intelligence models capable of summarizing news.\par \par \uc2 The eliminated positions are across the 500-person company, including in the newsroom, according to a person familiar with the matter. It is the first time the company has instituted layoffs.\par \par \uc2 Axios was started in 2017 by Mr. VandeHei and his business partners \uc2 Mike Allen\uc2  and Roy Schwartz, all of whom previously worked at Politico, the political news site. The publication quickly made a name for itself with its bullet-point format, distilling the news down into bite-size chunks.\par \par \uc2 In 2022, Axios was sold to \uc2 Cox Enterprises\uc2  in a deal valuing the media outlet at $525 million. The three founders continue to run the company.\par \par \uc2 Mr. VandeHei said in the email to staff on Tuesday that moving forward, Axios would increase its focus on U.S. news coverage and more rapidly expand its city-specific newsletters to new locations. He also said the company would continue building its paid subscription product, Axios Pro, which is aimed at business professionals.\par \par \uc2 Axios is also continuing to explore potential acquisitions.\par \par \uc2 Mr. VandeHei told The Times earlier this year that the rise of A.I. had influenced his thinking on how to position his media company, developing a thesis that the only outlets that would survive the technology were ones that had journalistic expertise, trusted content and in-person human connection. Mr. VandeHei said at the time that Axios would increase the number of live events it holds as well as build paid memberships around some of its star journalists.\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page B3. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240807ek870003m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
