\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Tech Bosses Preach Patience as They Spend and Spend on A.I.\b0\par\par\uc2 By Karen Weise \par \uc2 1,388 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 August 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Big technology companies show no signs of slowing their spending on artificial intelligence, even though a payoff still looks a long way away.\par \par \uc2 Mark Zuckerberg\uc2 , \uc2 Meta\uc2 's chief executive, started 2023 by declaring it the ''year of efficiency.'' Like several of its big tech peers, \uc2 Meta\uc2  cut jobs and mothballed expansion plans.\par \par \uc2 Then came A.I.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Zuckerberg started this year saying his company would spend more than $30 billion in 2024 on new tech infrastructure. In April, he raised that to $35 billion. On Wednesday, he increased it to at least $37 billion. And he said \uc2 Meta\uc2  would spend even more next year.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Zuckerberg said he'd rather build too fast ''rather than too late,'' and allow his competitors to get a big lead in the A.I. race.\par \par \uc2 The tech industry's biggest companies have made it clear over the last week that they have no intention of throttling their stunning levels of spending on artificial intelligence, even though investors are getting worried that a big payoff is further down the line than once thought.\par \par \uc2 In the last quarter alone, \uc2 Apple\uc2 , \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , \uc2 Meta\uc2 , \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  and \uc2 Google\uc2 's parent company \uc2 Alphabet\uc2  spent a combined $59 billion on capital expenses, 63 percent more than a year earlier and 161 percent more than four years ago. A large part of that was funneled into building data centers and packing them with new computer systems to build artificial intelligence. Only \uc2 Apple\uc2  has not dramatically increased spending because it does not build the most advanced A.I. systems itself.\par \par \uc2 If investors are getting anxious, they're going to have to learn to cope with their nerves. Last week, \uc2 Alphabet\uc2 's share price dropped more than 5 percent after it reported a 91 percent increase in capital expenses. But \uc2 Sundar Pichai\uc2 , \uc2 Alphabet\uc2 's chief executive, made the case for patience.\par \par \uc2 ''These things take time,'' he said, and ''the risk of underinvesting is dramatically greater than the risk of overinvesting.''\par \par \uc2 The leaders of the biggest tech companies see a once-in-a-generation opportunity in the generative A.I. technology behind the popular chatbots like ChatGPT. They believe it can revolutionize everything from the software that runs the complex operations of global companies to research on new drugs.\par \par \uc2 When ChatGPT debuted in late 2022, tech giants were beginning to dial back a burst of spending from the pandemic. But the industry's brief embrace of austerity went out the window when they saw the potential of artificial intelligence.\par \par \uc2 This new wave of A.I. is wildly expensive. The systems work with vast amounts of data and require sophisticated computer chips and new data centers to develop the technology and serve it to customers. The companies are seeing some sales from their A.I. work, but it is barely moving the needle financially.\par \par \uc2 In recent months, several high-profile tech industry watchers, including \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 's head of equity research and a partner at the venture firm Sequoia Capital, have questioned when or if A.I. will ever produce enough benefit to bring in the sales needed to cover its staggering costs. It is not clear that A.I. will come close to having the same impact as the internet or mobile phones, Goldman's Jim Covello wrote in a June report.\par \par \uc2 ''What $1 trillion problem will A.I. solve?'' he wrote. ''Replacing low wage jobs with tremendously costly technology is basically the polar opposite of the prior technology transitions I've witnessed in my 30 years of closely following the tech industry.''\par \par \uc2 Google\uc2 's parent company was the first big tech outfit to report its earnings for April through June, and it added weight to those overspending concerns. Though \uc2 Alphabet\uc2  had a 29 percent jump in profit, sales for ads on \uc2 YouTube\uc2 , which \uc2 Google\uc2  owns, were lower than expected and the massive jump in infrastructure spending -- \uc2 Google\uc2  spent an average of $145 million each day in the quarter -- rattled investors.\par \par \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  was up next. As \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 's largest investor, it had a jump on its peers and had been raising its capital spending every quarter since the start of last year. On Tuesday, \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  had a bit of unwelcome news: Its cloud computing business, where most of that A.I. work was being done, didn't grow as fast as expected.\par \par \uc2 But instead of serving as a moment of caution, the miss (about one percentage point below what was expected) added fuel to the building frenzy. Executives said that \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  had more demand for A.I. than they could serve from their data centers, a problem that they expect will persist through the end of the year. That helped explain why they are building so furiously.\par \par \uc2 Satya Nadella\uc2 , \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 's chief executive, said much of the capital spending went to acquire physical land and buildings, which they need to lock up in advance. But the remaining 60 percent was for what he called ''the kit,'' the expensive chips and other components of a computer network.\par \par \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 's executives also asked for patience, saying the spending would bring in revenue over a long time -- ''over 15 years and beyond,'' said \uc2 Amy Hood\uc2 , the company's financial chief. Digging into \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 's numbers indicates that the company is on track for more than $5 billion in sales for generative A.I. products this year. But for a tech giant that just reported $245 billion in annual revenue, that's still tiny.\par \par \uc2 (The \uc2 New York Times\uc2  has sued \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  and \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 , claiming copyright infringement of news content related to A.I. systems. The two companies have denied the suit's claims.)\par \par \uc2 The next day, \uc2 Meta\uc2  increased its predictions for how much it would spend. Mr. Zuckerberg said he was planning for the next generation of A.I. systems, and the next major update to their main A.I. model will demand 10 times more computing power.\par \par \uc2 Meta\uc2  gives away the advanced A.I. systems it develops, but Mr. Zuckerberg still said it was worth it. ''Part of what's important about A.I. is that it can be used to improve all of our products in almost every way,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, \uc2 Amazon\uc2  told investors it had spent more than $30 billion on capital expenses in the first half of the year, and that it too would spend more in the rest of the year. Executives said they need to balance building enough to meet demand without getting ahead of what they really need.\par \par \uc2 ''The reality right now is that while we're investing a significant amount in the A.I. space and in infrastructure, we would like to have more capacity than we already have today,'' said \uc2 Andy Jassy\uc2 , \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's chief executive. ''I mean, we have a lot of demand right now.''\par \par \uc2 That means buying land, building data centers and all the computers, chips and gear that go into them. \uc2 Amazon\uc2  executives put a positive spin on all that spending. ''We use that to drive revenue and free cash flow for the next decade and beyond,'' said \uc2 Brian Olsavsky\uc2 , the company's finance chief.\par \par \uc2 There are plenty of signs the boom will persist. In mid-July, \uc2 Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing Company\uc2 , which makes most of the in-demand chips designed by \uc2 Nvidia\uc2  that are used in A.I. systems, said those chips would be in scarce supply until the end of 2025.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Zuckerberg said A.I.'s potential is ''super exciting.''\par \par \uc2 ''It's why there are all the jokes about how all the tech C.E.O.s get on these earnings calls,'' he said, ''and just talk about A.I. the whole time.''\par \par \uc2 Mike Isaac and Tripp Mickle contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Mike Isaac and Tripp Mickle contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Mark Zuckerberg, who increased Meta's budget to $37 billion this week, said spending too little on A.I. right now is a bigger risk than spending too much. (PHOTOGRAPH BY LOREN ELLIOTT FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (B1); Alphabet's chief executive, Sundar Pichai, above, said 60 percent of the company's capital spending was for what he called ''the kit,'' the expensive components of a computer network. (PHOTOGRAPH BY JEFF CHIU/ASSOCIATED PRESS) (B4) This article appeared in print on page B1, B4. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240803ek830004b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
