\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Intel to Cut Over 15,000 Jobs As It Battles in the A.I. Market\b0\par\par\uc2 By Don Clark \par \uc2 949 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 August 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The Silicon Valley chip maker also reported a net loss and declining revenue in the latest quarter.\par \par \uc2 Intel, the Silicon Valley chip maker, said on Thursday that it would slash more than 15,000 jobs to aid a turnaround plan, as the company tries to recover after a series of stumbles.\par \par \uc2 The job cuts amount to 15 percent of Intel's work force. The company also announced other restructuring moves and a reduction in capital spending, which are expected to cut costs by $10 billion in 2025. To conserve cash, Intel said, it will suspend its quarterly dividend in the fourth quarter.\par \par \uc2 ''This is painful news for me to share,'' \uc2 Patrick Gelsinger\uc2 , Intel's chief executive, said in a letter to employees. ''I know it will be even more difficult for you to read. This is an incredibly hard day for Intel as we are making some of the most consequential changes in our company's history.''\par \par \uc2 The company's stock fell more than 20 percent in after-hours trading.\par \par \uc2 Intel, which produces microprocessor chips that serve as electronic brains in most computers, has battled a slump amid stiff competition in chips used for artificial intelligence. Its last major restructuring was in 2016, when the company said it would cut up to 12,000 jobs, or 11 percent of its work force.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Gelsinger has worked to reinvigorate the company after being named its top leader in early 2021. Among other actions, he quickly moved to become a top industry lobbyist for federal subsidies to encourage more U.S. production of the foundational components.\par \par \uc2 He has also tried to fix Intel's manufacturing issues. Unlike most of its peers, Intel makes chips as well as designs them. Others rely on outside production services called foundries, with most turning to \uc2 Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing Company\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Before Mr. Gelsinger became Intel's chief executive, \uc2 TSMC\uc2  had surpassed it in delivering regular advances in production technology that gives chips more computing power. He embarked on a costly effort to deliver five new technology generations -- which historically arrived every two years or so -- in four years. That effort is producing results, but with heavy costs.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Gelsinger also launched a parallel plan to build more factories and turn Intel into a major foundry making chips for others. That strategy helped Intel become the largest beneficiary in March of U.S. legislation known as the CHIPS Act, tentatively winning federal grants totaling $8.5 billion.\par \par \uc2 Besides manufacturing issues, Intel ran into product problems. Demand for personal computers that use its chips slumped last year. At the same time, customers have turned to rival \uc2 Nvidia\uc2  for A.I. chips for data centers, even as Intel has responded to the A.I. boom by adding features to its standard chips and offering a special-purpose chip line called Gaudi. \uc2 Advanced Micro Devices\uc2 , another rival, has also grabbed market share in standard data center chips and new products tailored for A.I.\par \par \uc2 Intel said on Thursday that revenue in its data center business fell 3 percent in the second quarter. In contrast, AMD on Tuesday reported a 115 percent jump for its data center business.\par \par \uc2 Overall, Intel swung to a loss of $1.6 billion in the second quarter, while revenue fell 1 percent to $12.8 billion. Its gross profit margin, a closely watched metric that once averaged around 65 percent, was 35.4 percent in the quarter.\par \par \uc2 In his letter to employees, Mr. Gelsinger said that revenue had not grown as expected, and that the company had yet to fully benefit from major trends like A.I. Intel posted about $24 billion less revenue last year than it did in 2020, yet its work force is 10 percent larger, he wrote.\par \par \uc2 ''There are a lot of reasons for this, but it's not a sustainable path forward,'' he wrote.\par \par \uc2 Beyond the costs, Mr. Gelsinger said Intel was still too bureaucratic and inefficient, despite past attempts to streamline operations. As part of the restructuring, he said, the company will trim the number of products it sells, stop nonessential work and reduce capital spending more than 24 percent. Many of the job cuts will be accomplished through early retirement and voluntary departure programs, he added.\par \par \uc2 The job cuts ''have challenged me to my core, and this is the hardest thing I've done in my career,'' Mr. Gelsinger wrote. He pledged that Intel would ''prioritize a culture of honesty, transparency and respect in the weeks and months to come.''\par \par \uc2 For the current quarter, Intel projected a gross profit margin of 34.5 percent and revenue of $12.5 billion to $13.5 billion, below analyst estimates of $14.3 billion.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Gelsinger and \uc2 David Zinsner\uc2 , Intel's chief financial officer, said on a conference call that one reason for the disappointing projection was the PC market. Computer makers have built up excess chip inventories as demand for such systems has slowed, particularly in China.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, Intel has bet that a new generation of A.I. PCs will drive more demand. The microprocessors it developed for such machines are composed of multiple chips, a large portion of which are manufactured by external foundries and cost Intel more than those produced in its own factories, Mr. Zinsner said. As Intel starts making such components internally, profit margins will improve, he added.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Gelsinger predicted that the investments in such components would pay off as A.I. PCs grew to more than half the PC market by 2026.\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page B4. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240802ek820003j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
