\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 New Union Leaders Elected at Amazon Warehouse\b0\par\par\uc2 By Noam Scheiber \par \uc2 745 \uc2 words\par \uc2 31 July 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 5\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The Amazon Labor Union has been divided over strategy and governance issues after winning a representation vote at a Staten Island warehouse in 2022.\par \par \uc2 A dissident group has won control of the Amazon Labor Union, the only union in the country that formally represents Amazon warehouse workers, election results on Tuesday showed.\par \par \uc2 The union won a representation vote at a Staten Island warehouse in 2022 but has yet to negotiate a contract as Amazon contests the outcome. The group has been divided over governance and strategy, as well as personality conflicts, after falling short in efforts to organize other Amazon facilities.\par \par \uc2 A leader of the dissident group, Connor Spence, will take over, succeeding the founding president, Christian Smalls, who chose not to run for re-election. Mr. Spence defeated the union's current recording secretary and a third candidate in an election that attracted roughly 250 votes, out of thousands of workers at the warehouse.\par \par \uc2 The result was announced by Mr. Spence's group and confirmed by Mr. Smalls.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Spence's group brought a lawsuit last year to force leadership elections within the union. The two sides announced a settlement in January that set the stage for this month's election, which was overseen by a court-approved monitor.\par \par \uc2 The dissident group, the A.L.U. Democratic Reform Caucus, argued that Mr. Smalls and other union leaders had too much power and were unaccountable to rank-and-file members, a charge that Mr. Smalls rejected.\par \par \uc2 The caucus also claimed victory for the union's three other officer positions. It said in a statement that after a long fight to reform the union, ''we are relieved to finally be able to turn our full attention toward bringing Amazon to the table.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Smalls said in a direct message that ''workers aren't interested in who's running the union,'' but ''want a contract to improve their quality of living.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Spence's group says it would survey workers at the Staten Island warehouse, known as JFK8, before setting priorities. But it has declared short-term goals like making it easier for workers to use cellphones on the job and winning better accommodations for pregnant workers, as well as longer-term goals like higher wages and more paid time off.\par \par \uc2 Amazon declined to comment on the election results but has said that its accommodations for pregnant workers meet or exceed state and federal laws and that it has invested billions in recent years to improve pay, benefits, training and safety.\par \par \uc2 The union's new leaders have said that getting Amazon to bargain will also require ''a robust national campaign which brings other Amazon warehouses like JFK8 into our network.''\par \par \uc2 The union will have a new ally in that fight: the 1.3-million-member \uc2 International Brotherhood of Teamsters\uc2 , with which the Amazon union formally affiliated in June. Under the agreement, the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  will provide the union with money and personnel to help with organizing, research, communications and legal matters.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Spence, a co-founder of the Amazon union, was fired last fall over what Amazon said were violations of its policy on off-duty access to the warehouse. He is challenging the firing before the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2  and is eligible to serve as union president during the challenge.\par \par \uc2 Members of the union say Amazon has been more aggressive in recent months enforcing rules that limit their ability to organize. The company called the police in June after Amazon Labor Union members and \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  gathered in front of the warehouse seeking to persuade workers to back the unions' affiliation agreement. After Mr. Spence and another Amazon union leader who had been fired refused to leave, officers handcuffed the two, took them to a police station and issued tickets requiring court appearances.\par \par \uc2 The company also brought in police during a mid-July protest on its Staten Island grounds. Mr. Spence was handcuffed and taken to the police station again, along with about half a dozen other protesters.\par \par \uc2 Amazon has said that employees are free to distribute material outside warehouses, and that it has called police when nonemployees create a disturbance and refuse to leave.\par \par \uc2 Amazon employees in line to vote in the parking lot of the JFK8 fulfillment center on Staten Island in 2022. (PHOTOGRAPH BY DESEAN MCCLINTON-HOLLAND FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page B5. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240731ek7v0003l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
