\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 U.S. Sues Contractor Over Sexual Abuse of Migrant Children\b0\par\par\uc2 By Aishvarya Kavi \par \uc2 906 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 July 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 20\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Southwest Key, the Texas-based nonprofit that is the largest provider of housing for unaccompanied minors who arrive at the southern border, has faced federal scrutiny before.\par \par \uc2 The Justice Department has sued Southwest Key Programs, the nation's largest operator of shelters for unaccompanied migrant children, accusing its employees of repeatedly subjecting minors in its care to sexual abuse and harassment.\par \par \uc2 Employees at Southwest Key, a nonprofit based in Austin, ''exploited the children's vulnerabilities, language barriers and distance from family and loved ones,'' according to the lawsuit, filed in federal court in Texas on Wednesday. From 2015 to at least 2023, children and teenagers at its shelters faced threats of violence intended to prevent them from reporting rape, solicitations of sex and entreaties for nude photographs, among other inappropriate conduct and abuse.\par \par \uc2 Southwest Key has worked with the federal government for more than two decades, playing a critical role in the shelter system. It has been awarded nearly $6 billion in federal funds since 2007, operating 29 shelters in Texas, Arizona and California that can house more than 6,300 children. The majority are 13 to 17 and come from Guatemala, Honduras and El Salvador. They receive meals and educational opportunities in such shelters until they can be placed with a parent, relative or other guardian.\par \par \uc2 Southwest Key documented dozens of incidents of abuse reported by children, but according to prosecutors, employees did not report abuse they had observed or knew of, or violations of policies designed to protect minors.\par \par \uc2 Anais Biera Miracle, a spokeswoman for Southwest Key, said it continued to shelter children and remained focused on ''the safety, health and well-being'' of the children in its care.\par \par \uc2 ''We continue to review the complaint, and it does not present the accurate picture of the care and commitment our employees provide to the youth and children,'' Ms. Miracle said in an email.\par \par \uc2 By one account, a worker who sexually abused three girls ages 5, 8 and 11 threatened to kill their families if they told. In another instance documented by Southwest Key, a supervisor deliberately changed shifts to be alone with a teenage girl he repeatedly raped, abused and threatened. At night, he would enter her bedroom and those of others in violation of Southwest Key's policies. The girl was transferred to a different shelter after she reported the abuse.\par \par \uc2 In yet another case, a Southwest Key clinician asked inappropriate questions about a girl's sexual experiences.\par \par \uc2 Southwest Key failed ''to act promptly to correct and end the sexual abuse and harassment,'' the Justice Department said in its complaint, which seeks compensation for the children who were abused.\par \par \uc2 The secretary of health and human services, \uc2 Xavier Becerra\uc2 , whose department contracts with Southwest Key, said in a statement on Thursday that the lawsuit raised ''serious pattern-or-practice concerns.''\par \par \uc2 ''H.H.S. has a zero-tolerance policy for all forms of sexual abuse, sexual harassment, inappropriate sexual behavior, and discrimination,'' Mr. Becerra said.\par \par \uc2 Caring for migrant children in federal custody is a lucrative, billion-dollar business with little transparency.\par \par \uc2 By law, children who arrive at the southern border with Mexico unaccompanied by parents or legal guardians are the responsibility of the federal government. The \uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\uc2  is then responsible for temporarily housing them, usually with a provider contracted to do so like Southwest Key.\par \par \uc2 Most unaccompanied migrant children who arrive in the United States have already endured a traumatic and treacherous journey north. But increasingly, watchdog groups have reported dangerous conditions at shelters run by government contractors.\par \par \uc2 It is not the first time Southwest Key has come under fire.\par \par \uc2 Southwest Key drew particular scrutiny when President Donald J. Trump enacted a family separation policy, forcing more than 5,000 children into federal custody after they were removed from their parents or guardians at the southern border. The separations, which largely took place in 2017 and 2018, were a financial boon to contractors like Southwest Key. Federal award money for the nonprofit more than doubled in that time.\par \par \uc2 In 2018, \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  revealed that Southwest Key had funneled government money through a web of for-profit companies to convert public funds into private money for the organization and pay top executives millions of dollars. Shortly after, the Justice Department opened an investigation into possible financial improprieties. Southwest Key also began an internal inquiry and high-level executives, including its founder and chief financial officer, eventually resigned.\par \par \uc2 Around the same time, Southwest Key also attracted attention over accounts of abuse at its shelters. In 2018, two of its workers were charged with sexually assaulting minors at Arizona facilities. That same year, videos emerged showing physical abuse at shelters in Arizona. Southwest Key paid a $73,000 fine and lost licenses for two shelters.\par \par \uc2 The health department at the time threatened to pull licenses for more shelters, underlining the complicated considerations at play by throwing into question the situation of thousands of migrant children. But the federal contracts continued to flow and the nonprofit remains the largest private provider of housing for unaccompanied migrant children arriving in the United States.\par \par \uc2 Casa Padre, a shelter for unaccompanied migrant children run by the nonprofit Southwest Key Programs, in Brownsville, Texas. (PHOOTGRAPH BY TAMIR KALIFA FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A20. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240722ek7k00006\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
