\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Union Workers at \uc2 Samsung\uc2  Launch an Indefinite Strike\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jin Yu Young and John Liu \par \uc2 682 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 July 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The tech giant's largest union escalated its dispute with management after failing to reach an agreement over pay and working policies.\par \par \uc2 Unionized workers at \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2  said Wednesday they would go on an indefinite strike, an escalation of a rare labor dispute that could disrupt the technology giant's world-leading chip business.\par \par \uc2 An estimated 6,500 workers walked off the job on Monday for a planned three-day strike over pay and working conditions. The Nationwide Samsung Electronics Union decided to extend the strike after ''hearing no word'' from the company, according to Lee Hyun Kuk, the vice president of the union, which represents more than 31,000 workers, or a quarter of the work force at \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Samsung\uc2 , South Korea's biggest private employer, has long been the world's largest maker of memory chips, which help computers and other electronics equipment store information. The company is also a leading manufacturer of logic chips, which make computers run, behind only \uc2 Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing Company\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The union said it has been negotiating with \uc2 Samsung\uc2  since January over vacation days and wages.\par \par \uc2 ''As the strike goes on, the management's blood will dry out and they will eventually come to the negotiating table on their knees,'' the union said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 The union said its work stoppage this week has slowed some \uc2 Samsung\uc2  operations and production. A \uc2 Samsung\uc2  representative said the strike has not affected production and it would work to avoid disruptions in the future. The company remained ''committed to engaging in good faith negotiations with the union,'' the representative said.\par \par \uc2 Because of the ''high level of automation in the factories and the low actual need for manual labor,'' the impact of the strike was expected to be minimal, said Avril Wu, a senior research vice president at TrendForce, a market research firm.\par \par \uc2 ''Our research team has consulted with buyers and sellers in the memory market, and neither side is particularly concerned about this issue now,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 In June, \uc2 Samsung\uc2  workers went on a one-day strike, the first in the company's history.\par \par \uc2 Union workers are demanding a wage increase by 3.5 percent, improved bonus policies and an extra day of paid vacation. Entry-level \uc2 Samsung\uc2  employees typically get over two weeks a year of vacation, some paid and some unpaid. The union also wants \uc2 Samsung\uc2  to agree to compensate workers for any lost wages during the strike.\par \par \uc2 While in recent years union workers have said that they received bonuses of as much as 30 percent, last year they got nothing. The average member earned about 80 million won last year, or around $60,000, before incentives, they said.\par \par \uc2 ''We won't go back until all of the demands are met,'' Mr. Lee said.\par \par \uc2 Last week, \uc2 Samsung\uc2  said that it would report a larger-than-expected jump in operating profit for the second quarter, of $7.5 billion. The company's stock has recently set a series of multiyear highs as demand for chips to power A.I. applications has soared.\par \par \uc2 Kim Jae Won has worked in the memory chip division at \uc2 Samsung\uc2  since 2019. ''I understand if the company is doing poorly they can't give us our bonuses,'' he said. ''But the fact that the executives took home huge bonuses last year, I can't understand.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Kim attended a rally of workers outside a \uc2 Samsung\uc2  semiconductor factory on Monday. ''I felt a sense of purpose and pride that thousands of members were there,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 For decades, \uc2 Samsung\uc2  was known for its aversion to organized labor, and unions have organized workers at the company only in the past several years.\par \par \uc2 Labor strikes in South Korea are not uncommon. Since February, over 10,000 doctors walked off the job in protest of government plans to increase the number of admitted medical students. Last spring, thousands of construction workers rallied over discontent with the country's labor policies.\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page B3. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240711ek7b0004i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
