\par\par \uc2 CALCULATOR\par \uc2 Real Estate Desk; SECTRE\par \b \uc2 Best Cities for New College Graduates\b0\par\par\uc2 By Matt Yan \par \uc2 401 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 June 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A recent study ranked 100 of the largest U.S. cities based on median rents, job opportunities and social metrics.\par \par \uc2 Recent college graduates have plenty on their minds this summer: The national unemployment rate ticked up last month, and a widening generational wealth gap is making it harder than ever to buy a first home. Where are they supposed to go now?\par \par \uc2 A new study by \uc2 Zumper\uc2  might help. It analyzed the country's 100 most populous cities to find the best ones for new graduates based on several metrics (where adequate data was available). They include median one-bedroom rents and restaurants per capita, as well as \uc2 U.S. Census Bureau\uc2  data on the population of 18-to-34 year olds, the number of 25-year-olds with bachelor's degrees, the number of single people, the median income of younger residents, and local unemployment rates.\par \par \uc2 Topping the list was Minneapolis, which had a reasonable median one-bedroom rent ($1,150 a month) and ranked 15th for its unemployment rate (5.2 percent). Oklahoma City's median rent ($849) was the lowest among the top 15 cities, helping it rank 10th overall. San Francisco ranked fifth thanks to its high median income ($87,946) for people 25 and younger -- which helps with its huge median rent ($2,895).\par \par \uc2 Laredo, Texas, fell to the bottom of the list with its small share of 25-year-olds with bachelor's degrees. It also ranked low in median income and the number of restaurants per capita. Its higher scores for median rent ($850) and unemployment rate (5.5 percent) weren't enough to make up for its downsides. Augusta, Ga., which ranked 91st for median income ($22,861) came in 99th overall. Providence, R.I., finished just one spot higher, ranking 31st for unemployment rate (7.1 percent), and much lower than that for its paltry population of young residents.\par \par \uc2 If you're thinking about New York City, which finished 20th overall, it had the highest median rent of any metro in the study. But it also had the largest population of residents between the ages of 18 and 34 (more than 2 million), and scored well for median income.\par \par \uc2 For weekly email updates on residential real estate news, sign up here.\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page RE2. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240623ek6n00040\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
