\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Compromise Heads Off Appeal of Law Letting Workers Sue Employers\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kurtis Lee \par \uc2 577 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 June 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Gov. \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  announced a deal with business and labor leaders heading off a ballot measure to repeal the law, which has cost companies billions.\par \par \uc2 A last-minute political compromise has headed off an effort to repeal a California law allowing workers to sue employers for workplace violations -- a legal tool that has cost companies billions of dollars.\par \par \uc2 The compromise, announced on Tuesday by Gov. \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2 , followed meetings with business leaders and the powerful California Labor Federation over ways to modify the 2004 law, the Private Attorneys General Act.\par \par \uc2 The law, known as PAGA, lets employees file civil complaints -- on their own behalf and for fellow workers -- against businesses, sometimes costing them tens of millions of dollars in settlements.\par \par \uc2 ''We came to the table and hammered out a deal that works for both businesses and workers, and it will bring needed improvements to this system,'' Mr. Newsom said in a statement on Tuesday. ''This proposal maintains strong protections for workers, provides incentives for businesses to comply with labor laws and reduces litigation.''\par \par \uc2 A study released in February by a coalition opposing the law found it had cost businesses around $10 billion since 2013. That same report found more than 3,000 proposed settlements under the law in 2022, a tenfold increase from 2016. (In most cases, the state records settlement proposals but not the amount ultimately paid.)\par \par \uc2 In 2023, \uc2 Google\uc2  settled for $27 million after employees used the law as their basis for accusing the tech company of unfair labor practices. And in 2018, \uc2 Walmart\uc2  employees won a settlement of $65 million after accusing the retailer of not providing sufficient seating for workers.\par \par \uc2 Business groups got a measure to repeal the law on the November ballot. They agreed to withdraw the measure once legislation reflecting the compromise is passed and signed into law.\par \par \uc2 Labor groups have cited the law as a necessary check on corporations.\par \par \uc2 A recent report from the U.C.L.A. Labor Center found that the prospective ballot measure would effectively eliminate ''one of California workers' strongest remaining tools for preventing and correcting wage theft and other workplace abuses,'' said Tia Koonse, the center's legal and policy research manager.\par \par \uc2 The compromise calls for, among other things, creating higher penalties on employers that flout labor laws and increasing the amount of penalty money that goes to employees to 35 percent from 25 percent. Moreover, it stipulates that any legal action must be initiated by the employee who experiences the violations described in the suit.\par \par \uc2 ''This package provides meaningful reforms that ensure workers continue to have a strong vehicle to get labor claims resolved, while also limiting the frivolous litigation that has cost employers billions without benefiting workers,'' \uc2 Jennifer Barrera\uc2 , president of the \uc2 California Chamber of Commerce\uc2 , said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 Lorena Gonzalez\uc2 , the leader of the California Labor Federation, said in a statement that her group was pleased ''to have negotiated reforms to PAGA that better ensure abusive practices by employers are cured and that workers are made whole, quicker.''\par \par \uc2 ''PAGA is an essential tool to help workers hold corporations accountable for widespread wage theft, safety violations and misclassification,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 California Gov. Gavin Newsom announced a compromise Tuesday that would modify the law allowing workers to sue employers for workplace violations. (PHOTOGRAPH BY RICH PEDRONCELLI/ASSOCIATED PRESS) This article appeared in print on page B2. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240620ek6k0003b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
