\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Unions Join in Fight for Amazon Workers\b0\par\par\uc2 By Noam Scheiber \par \uc2 1,297 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 June 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 An affiliation agreement between the Amazon Labor Union and the 1.3 million-member \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  signals an escalation in challenging the online retailer.\par \par \uc2 After years of organizing \uc2 Amazon\uc2  workers and pressuring the company to bargain over wages and working conditions, two prominent unions are teaming up to challenge the online retailer.\par \par \uc2 The partnership was made final after members of the Amazon Labor Union, the only union formally representing \uc2 Amazon\uc2  warehouse workers in the United States, overwhelmingly chose to affiliate with the 1.3-million-member \uc2 International Brotherhood of Teamsters\uc2  in voting that ended Monday. The vote was overseen by the \uc2 Amazon\uc2  union.\par \par \uc2 The A.L.U. scored a surprise victory in an election at a Staten Island warehouse in 2022. But it has yet to begin bargaining with \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , which continues to contest the election outcome. Leaders of both unions said the affiliation agreement would put them in a better position to challenge \uc2 Amazon\uc2  and would provide the A.L.U. with more money and staff support.\par \par \uc2 ''The \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  and A.L.U. will fight fearlessly to ensure \uc2 Amazon\uc2  workers secure the good jobs and safe working conditions they deserve in a union contract,'' Sean O'Brien, the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  president, said in a statement early Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  declined to comment on the affiliation.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  are ramping up their efforts to organize \uc2 Amazon\uc2  workers nationwide. The union voted to create an \uc2 Amazon\uc2  division in 2021, and Mr. O'Brien was elected that year partly on a platform of making inroads at the company.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  told the A.L.U. that they had allocated $8 million to support organizing at \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , according to Christian Smalls, the A.L.U. president, and that the larger union was prepared to tap its more than $300 million strike and defense fund to aid in the effort. The \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  did not comment on their budget for organizing at \uc2 Amazon\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  also recently reached an affiliation agreement with workers organizing at \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's largest airplane hub in the United States, a Kentucky facility known as KCVG. Experts have said unionizing KCVG could give workers substantial leverage because \uc2 Amazon\uc2  relies heavily on the hub to meet its one- and two-day shipping goals.\par \par \uc2 David Levin, staff director for \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  for a Democratic Union, a reform group within the union that helped mobilize \uc2 United Parcel Service\uc2  workers during last year's successful contract campaign, said many \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  members who were involved in pressuring UPS were now helping \uc2 Amazon\uc2  workers organize.\par \par \uc2 ''Worker-leaders and activists are coming out of the UPS contract campaign and getting involved in building \uc2 Amazon\uc2  volunteer organizing committees,'' Mr. Levin said.\par \par \uc2 Efforts to unionize \uc2 Amazon\uc2  over the past decade have been scattered across a variety of established unions and independent worker groups. Some experts argue that given the company's size and longstanding opposition to unions, establishing a significant union presence there will require some consolidation of the organizing.\par \par \uc2 ''We've had these different efforts, all these different pockets, that yielded some important breakthroughs,'' said Barry Eidlin, a sociologist at \uc2 McGill University in Montreal\uc2  who studies labor. ''But they also revealed the limitations of having a diffuse approach.''\par \par \uc2 The affiliation agreement with the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2 , a copy of which was shared with \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 , stipulates that the A.L.U. will have the exclusive right within the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  to organize additional \uc2 Amazon\uc2  warehouse workers in New York City and promises to help the new local with organizing, research, communications and legal representation.\par \par \uc2 It also gives the A.L.U. a role in the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2 ' broader \uc2 Amazon\uc2  organizing, stating that at least three members of the local will take part in ''executive planning and strategy discussions'' of the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2 ' \uc2 Amazon\uc2  division, and that the local will ''lend its expertise to assist in organizing other \uc2 Amazon\uc2  facilities'' across the country.\par \par \uc2 The A.L.U. energized the labor movement with its victory in 2022, but it soon encountered major challenges. It lost a union election at a nearby warehouse on Staten Island a few weeks later and another election at a warehouse near Albany, N.Y., that fall.\par \par \uc2 The union began to fracture after the second loss, with several A.L.U. organizers raising concerns that the union's leaders had too much power and were unaccountable to members. Mr. Smalls asserted that the union was worker-led.\par \par \uc2 An A.L.U. dissident group critical of Mr. Smalls filed a lawsuit in 2023 seeking to force leadership elections. The two sides announced a settlement in January, and elections are scheduled for the summer, to be overseen by a monitor approved by a federal court. Mr. Smalls is not a candidate, while the dissident group, the A.L.U. Democratic Reform Caucus, is fielding candidates for all four leadership positions. The slate is led by Connor Spence, an A.L.U. founder.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, the A.L.U. has faced financial difficulties and ended last year with $33,000 in assets and $81,000 in liabilities, according to federal filings.\par \par \uc2 In May, both A.L.U. factions visited the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  headquarters in Washington, where \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  officials pitched them on the idea of affiliating, Mr. Smalls said.\par \par \uc2 He said the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  had offered to make their resources available to \uc2 Amazon\uc2  workers -- including strike pay -- while largely preserving the \uc2 Amazon\uc2  union's independence. He signed the affiliation agreement in early June.\par \par \uc2 The signing surprised the reform caucus, which had told the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  that A.L.U. members would need more time to deliberate. But the caucus ultimately decided to back affiliation as long as A.L.U. members ratified it, saying it would help ''turn the beachhead we've secured in Staten Island into a militant, autonomous local.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Spence, the reform caucus candidate for A.L.U. president, said that if his group won the leadership election on Staten Island, it would put together a plan to take on \uc2 Amazon\uc2  in consultation with workers and present the plan to the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  in hopes of securing the necessary resources.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  fired Mr. Spence last fall for what it said were violations of its policy governing off-duty access to its facilities. He is challenging the firing in a case that is before an administrative judge with the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Mr. Spence and another fired \uc2 Amazon\uc2  worker were removed by the police last week after they appeared in front of the warehouse trying to persuade workers to ratify the affiliation agreement. Officers handcuffed the two former workers, took them to a station and gave them tickets requiring a court appearance.\par \par \uc2 Lisa Levandowski, an \uc2 Amazon\uc2  spokeswoman, said the company had called the police because a group, mostly \uc2 Teamsters\uc2 , was creating a disturbance outside the warehouse and had rebuffed \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's request to leave. She said that after the police arrived, all but Mr. Spence and his former co-worker had left. (Employees are allowed to distribute material outside the building during off hours.)\par \par \uc2 Mr. Spence said he had appeared in front of the building many times for organizing purposes in recent weeks without encountering the police.\par \par \uc2 Amazon workers lined up for a unionization vote on Staten Island in 2022. (GRPAH BY DESEAN MCCLINTON-HOLLAND FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (B1); The Amazon Labor Union scored a surprise victory on Staten Island in 2022, but Amazon continues to contest the outcome. Leaders of the Teamsters and the A.L.U. said their agreement puts them in a better position to challenge Amazon and provide the A.L.U. with more money and staff support. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY CHANG W. LEE/THE NEW YORK TIMES; DESEAN MCCLINTON-HOLLAND FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (B3) This article appeared in print on page B1, B3. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240619ek6j0003s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
