\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Tesla\uc2  Investors In Scandinavia Seek Respect Of Labor Rights\b0\par\par\uc2 By Melissa Eddy \par \uc2 985 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 June 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 6\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Tesla\uc2  mechanics in Sweden have been striking for six months with little movement from their employer. Nordic shareholders hope to change that.\par \par \uc2 The fight over \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 's pay package and the vote over whether to move \uc2 Tesla\uc2 's headquarters to Texas have been the main focus of attention ahead of the company's shareholders meeting on Thursday, but not for investors from Nordic countries.\par \par \uc2 Tesla\uc2 's major shareholders in Sweden, Denmark and Norway are instead looking to the meeting to bring the issue of labor rights at the automaker to the fore.\par \par \uc2 Behind the campaign is the strike of \uc2 Tesla\uc2 's mechanics in Sweden. Now stretching into its sixth month, the dispute has drawn in unions from across the region that have joined in blockades aimed at bringing the U.S. carmaker to the negotiating table to reach a collective agreement with its Swedish workers.\par \par \uc2 Several of the biggest shareholders in the Nordic countries are urging others to back a proposal that would require \uc2 Tesla\uc2  to respect the right of workers to assemble.\par \par \uc2 Tesla\uc2  did not respond to a request for comment. It has remained resistant to unions in Europe, even in countries with strong traditions of organized labor. Mr. Musk has expressed his disdain for organized labor. ''I disagree with the idea of unions,'' he said at the DealBook Summit in New York last year.\par \par \uc2 None of \uc2 Tesla\uc2 's factories are unionized, potentially giving the company an advantage over rivals like \uc2 Ford Motor\uc2 , \uc2 General Motors\uc2  and \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  that must pay union wages. But in the United States, \uc2 Tesla\uc2  is a prime target for the \uc2 United Automobile Workers union\uc2 , which is in a strong position after winning the largest wage increases in years recently for workers at unionized plants.\par \par \uc2 The proposal up for vote on Thursday was put forward by Folksam Group, an insurance company from Sweden, along with a Canadian pension fund and equity funds from the United States. Together they are calling on \uc2 Tesla\uc2 's board to adopt a policy that would commit to ''noninterference and good faith bargaining in accordance with internationally recognized human rights standards with respect to freedom of association and collective bargaining,'' according to a filing with the \uc2 U.S. Securities and Exchange Commission\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Among those backing the proposal, which is up for vote on Thursday, are KLP, Norway's biggest pension fund, which holds 900,000 \uc2 Tesla\uc2  shares worth some 1.7 billion Norwegian crowns, or $162 million.\par \par \uc2 ''We as a long-term investor expect that companies we invest in will adhere to the highest international standards in its business operations, in particular when they have business operations across the globe where the standards may vary from country to country,'' said Kiran Aziz, KLP's head of responsible investments.\par \par \uc2 The Norwegian fund was one of several investors from the Nordic countries to send a letter to \uc2 Tesla\uc2 's management last year addressing the issue of labor rights.\par \par \uc2 Despite the strike and the campaign by those Nordic shareholders, Mr. Musk enjoys a strong following among many of the Sweden's most tech-savvy drivers. Although a majority of Swedes have expressed support for the strike, many are fans of Mr. Musk. \uc2 Tesla\uc2  sold 20,400 cars in Sweden last year, and the Model Y is the country's best seller.\par \par \uc2 Tesla\uc2  mechanics who are members of the \uc2 IF Metall\uc2  union walked off their jobs in late October. Dozens remain on strike and the union has been picketing the company's facilities across Sweden.\par \par \uc2 The union say \uc2 Tesla\uc2  is flouting Sweden's tradition of collective agreements. About 90 percent of Swedish workers are covered by these agreements, which also apply to nonunion employees and set work conditions across industries.\par \par \uc2 Tesla\uc2  has refused to engage in the process of collective bargaining that sets such an agreement, arguing that the company pays its workers competitively and complies with local labor laws.\par \par \uc2 That has not stopped the union from rallying more than a dozen other labor organizations to support its side through sympathy measures, which are allowed under Swedish law. For several months, workers from other industries have been refusing to provide \uc2 Tesla\uc2  with services ranging from unloading of its cars at ports, to trash removal to the delivery of new license plates.\par \par \uc2 Last month, Sweden's largest union, Unionen, joined the effort with a walkout by workers who carry out equipment inspections for the company.\par \par \uc2 Tesla\uc2  has so far found workarounds and has managed to keep its business going without significant disruptions for customers.\par \par \uc2 KLP, the Norwegian pension fund, has also said that it would vote against Mr. Musk's $46.5 billion pay package. Although the vote on pay is unrelated to the labor dispute, the fund said that ''the total award value remains excessive'' despite \uc2 Tesla\uc2 's significant growth. KLP also voted against the pay package in 2018.\par \par \uc2 Major shareholders appear divided over whether to support the pay package, which was nullified by a judge at a Delaware court in January. \uc2 Tesla\uc2  is now asking shareholders to approve the pay package and Mr. Musk has been lobbying shareholders for support over the social media platform X, which he owns.\par \par \uc2 In reaction to a post by Mr. Musk urging small investors to be allowed to vote, some Swedish retail brokerage firms broke with tradition in Europe by saying they would allow their customers to cast votes at the annual general meeting on Thursday, after they received requests to do so.\par \par \uc2 ''We always want to do our best to meet our customers' requests, and decided to make an exception this time and to make it possible for them to vote at this specific \uc2 Tesla\uc2  annual general meeting,'' said Sofia Svavar, a spokeswoman for \uc2 Avanza\uc2 , an online bank based in Stockholm.\par \par \uc2 Jack Ewing contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Jack Ewing contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page B6. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240613ek6d0005a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
